resistivity is the physical magnitude showing the largest range of values, with differences of ca. 24 orders of magnitude from silver to quartz). Even more, for a given sample, the thermal variation may induce changes of several orders of magnitude in the resistivity. A second problem associated with the conductivity measurements deals with the effect of the electrical contacts with the sample. Thus, if the sample resistivity is very low, the instrument will mainly measure the sample-wire contact resistance. On the contrary, if the sample’s resistivity is high, the contact resistance will be negligible (as observed in most CPs) but if it is very high the experiment may only measure the internal impedance of the voltmeter and leakage currents. An additional problem affecting the conductivity measurements is the lack of homogeneity and the anisotropy of many samples. This anisotropy may be inherent (low dimensional structures) or due to the shape of the sample (single crystals, pressed pellets, thin films,. . .).
All these difficulties have led to the design of different measuring methods depending on the nature and geometry of the sample and on the magnitude of its conductivity. Thus, when the resistivity is very low, to avoid the effects of contacts resistance, the best way is to use the four contacts method (Scheme 1).
This method involves the connection of four parallel in-line contacts to the sample. The two external contacts are used to apply a current $\cdot{}$ and $I-$ ) and the two internal ones are used to measure the voltage drop across the sample ( $\stackrel{\cdot}{V}+$ and $V-$ ). The connection between the thin metallic wires (usually tempered Cu, Ag, Au or $\mathrm{\mathbf{P}}\mathrm{t}$ wires with diameters of $25\mathrm{-}100~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ and the sample are done with the help of some metallic emulsion of fine powders in an organic solvent (silver, gold, platinum or graphite pastes) or simply by pressing the wires against the sample (if the mechanical resistance of the sample allows this last method). The resistivity is calculated from the cross-sectional area $(A\:=\:a\times d)$ and the distance between the internal contacts $(l)$ as $\rho=R\times(a\times d/l)$ . The four contacts method is more accurate since it only measures the resistance of the sample $(R_{\mathrm{x}})$ but neither the resistance of the wires nor that of the contacts as long as the internal impedance of the voltmeter is higher than the sample resistance. In contrast, in the two probe method the resistance of the sample and those of the wires and contacts are added in the measured resistance. Since these wire and contact resistances are usually of a few Ohms, only for highly conducting samples these resistances have to be considered. The only (sometimes important) advantage of the two-probe method is the smaller limiting sample size that can be measured with two contacts (typically above ca. $50~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ vs. ca. $250~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ for the four contacts method). When the samples are very thin or narrow or are not regular, the measurement of the sample dimensions may imply an important error, leading to errors in the calculated resistivity. In these cases, the four-points (different from the four contacts) and the van der Pauw methods are more convenient. In the first case, the four in-line contacts are very small equidistant points, separated by a distance ( $\mathit{l})$ (Scheme 2). Again the two external contacts are used to apply a current $(I)$ and the two internal ones to measure the voltage drop $(V)$ . The resistivity can be easily calculated as: $\rho=2\pi\times l\times(V/I)$ .
The other method used for thin samples is the van der Pauw configuration where the four contacts are glued in the periphery of the thin plate-like sample (whose thickness is $d,$ approximately arranged in the vertex of a square (Scheme 2). The resistivity in this case is calculated using the formula:
$$
\rho={\frac{\pi d}{\ln2}}{\frac{R_{\mathrm{a}}+R_{\mathrm{b}}}{2}}f(R_{\mathrm{a}}/R_{\mathrm{b}})
$$
where $R_{\mathrm{a}}$ is the resistance measured along one of the sides of the square $(R_{\mathrm{a}}=V_{\mathrm{AB}}/I_{\mathrm{CD}}$ , being $V_{\mathrm{AB}}$ the voltage drop across A and B when the current passes from $\mathrm{^c}$ to $\mathrm{\DeltaD}$ ) and $R_{\mathrm{b}}$ the resistance along the other (orthogonal) side $(R_{\mathrm{b}}=\mathrm{\nabla}V_{\mathrm{AD}}/I_{\mathrm{BC}},$ being $V_{\mathrm{AD}}$ the voltage drop across A and $\mathrm{~D~}$ when the current passes from B to $\mathbf{C}$ ), the resistance is measured by applying a current along one side and measuring the $V$ drop across the parallel one. The function $f(R_{\mathrm{a}}/R_{\mathrm{b}})$ depends on the resistances ratio in a sigmoidal way.9,10 In the ideal case of an isotropic square sample $(R_{\mathrm{a}}~=~R_{\mathrm{b}}~=~R)$ , the van der Pauw equation reduces to:
$$
\rho=\frac{\pi d}{\ln2}R
$$
As already mentioned, an additional problem deals with the lack of homogeneity in many samples (either arising from the shape anisotropy or from the structure of the compound). The shape anisotropy can be well resolved with the van der Pauw and the four points methods.11 Albeit, the inherent anisotropy of the sample requires the use of the Montgomery method or, if possible, the measurement of the resistivity along different directions or on different faces of the crystals in order to have a complete idea of the electrical anisotropy. The Montgomery method allows the determination of the resistivity along two different directions in anisotropic samples with a rectangular prismatic shape (Scheme 3).
This method requires four contacts in the four corners of one of the faces of the prisms (with dimensions $a$ and $b$ ). The resistivities in the two directions ( $\dot{\rho}_{a}$ and $\rho_{b})$ are calculated with the equations:
$$
\sqrt{\rho_{a}\rho_{b}}=\frac{a}{b}f\Big(R_{\mathrm{b}}\Big/_{R_{\mathrm{a}}}\Big)\mathrm{and}\sqrt{\rho_{a}\rho_{b}}=c R_{\mathrm{a}}f^{\prime}\Big(b_{/a}\Big)
$$
where $a,\ b$ and $c$ are the three dimensions of the prism, $R_{\mathrm{a}}(=V_{34}/I_{12})$ and $R_{\mathrm{b}}(=V_{13}/I_{24})$ are the resistances measured along a and $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{b}}$ , respectively and $f(R_{\mathrm{b}}/R_{\mathrm{a}})$ and $f(b/a)$ are functions that vary exponentially with the ratios $R_{\mathrm{b}}/R_{\mathrm{a}}$ and $b/a$ , respectively.11 When the sample is a powder the crystal anisotropy is removed but in these cases the conductivity has to be measured on a pressed pellet. Unfortunately, in these cases the resistivity is mainly dominated by the intergrain boundary contacts, which tend to increase the resistivity (usually between one and three orders of magnitude) acting as gaps that block the conductivity or as carrier accumulators. This behaviour implies that true metallic materials may appear as low band-gap semiconductors or semimetals if they are measured as pressed pellets. In these cases, the application of pressure to form the pellets may also induce irreversible transitions that would change the resistivity values and/or their thermal behaviour.
Another source of errors in the determination of the exact value of the conductivity is its high sensitivity to the quality of the crystals. This problem may lead to significant differences in the conductivity measured in different crystals of the same sample. Furthermore, in many molecular superconductors the quality of the crystal may determine the critical temperature and even the presence or absence of the superconducting transition. Of course, the use of several crystals (at least three of four) allows a more precise determination of the value, and the necessary reliability in the observed value.
Finally, it is worth to mention that there are other less common problems such as the conduction through humidity layers in the sample and the possible reaction of the organic solvent or the paste with the sample that may affect the conductivity measurements. These and other problems are, fortunately, very scarce and, together with their possible solutions, have already been discussed in detail.12
## 2.2. Mechanisms of the electrical conductivity in CPs and low dimensional conductors
Usually resistivity measurements are performed as a function of temperature (from room temperature down to a few K) since the thermal variation of the conductivity is more informative and more reliable than the room temperature value. Thus, although in a first approach the different electrical behaviours can be distinguished looking at the room temperature value of the conductivity (which is usually in the range $10^{1}{-}10^{5}\ \mathrm{S\cm}^{-1}$ for metals, $10^{-10}{-}10^{1}\ \mathrm{S}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for semiconductors and below ca. $10^{-10}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ for insulators), the limits between the different regimes are not clear.12 The only reliable way to obtain a clear picture of the electrical behaviour of a compound is to perform the thermal variation of the electrical conductivity inside the Ohmic regime. These measurements show an increase of the conductivity (or decrease of the resistivity) with decreasing temperature in metals and the opposite behaviour in semiconductors (and insulators). Note that although semiconductors show all a thermally activated conductivity (the conductivity increases with increasing temperature), the temperature dependence is not the same in all cases since there are different conduction mechanisms.
In general, thermally activated semiconductors present a small gap between the conduction and valence bands (in fact, the classical insulator definition is applied when the band gap is above $3\mathrm{eV}$ ). When this gap is very small, in a first approach we can assume that the number of carriers and, hence, the conductivity, should show an exponential dependence with temperature $(\sigma\propto T^{a})$ . This is the case for some conducting polymers with low doping levels, where values as high as 14 can be found for the exponent $a$ .13 In classical semiconductors the thermal dependence of the conductivity follows the Arrhenius law: $\sigma(T)~=~\sigma_{0}\exp(-E_{\mathrm{a}}/k T)$ , where $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ represents the activation energy (corresponding to half of the band gap, $E_{\mathrm{a}}=E_{\mathrm{g}}/2\rangle$ and $\sigma_{0}$ is a pre-exponential factor. Classical semiconductors are quite common among crystalline semiconductors but when dealing with amorphous materials, conducting polymers or coordination polymers, the conduction mechanism may change from a band model to a localized one. In this localized model charges move through a so-called hopping mechanism, which is the term used to describe the ‘‘jump’’ of charges through a phonon-assisted quantum tunnelling mechanism.12,14 The so-called hopping model describes the conductivity with a modified exponential law: $\sigma=\sigma_{0}\exp[-(T_{0}/T)^{a}]$ , where the exponent $a$ is related to the electronic dimensionality of the sample, $a=1/(1+d)$ , being $d$ the dimensionality. Thus, for one, two and three dimensional systems the $a$ value is $1/2,1/3$ and $1/4$ , respectively. Note that for three dimensional systems $a=1/4$ and we obtain Mott’s law.15 In this expression, $\sigma_{0}$ and $T_{0}$ are related to the electronic density of the states at the Fermi level, $N(\mathrm{F})$ , to the so-called localization length and to the phonons frequency. This variable range hopping (VRH) model has been used to explain conductivity data of many conducting polymers as well as coordination polymers (the term ‘‘variable’’ arises from the fact that the hopping range varies with temperature, since the thermal energy of the electrons and the phonon frequency change with temperature). Unfortunately in some cases the data can be fit to different $a$ values (precluding a precise determination of the electronic dimensionality) or the obtained $a$ value is located between two limiting cases (i.e., when the hopping mechanism appears mainly, but not exclusively, in one or two dimensions).
Besides the hopping mechanism, in the case of doped conducting polymers and $\pi$ -conjugated systems there are two additional different mechanisms to explain conductivity: polarons/bipolarons and solitons. Polarons are charged carriers that bear a spin whereas bipolarons are pairs of compensated spins. When the doping level is low, the main carriers are polarons in conducting polymers with non-degenerate ground states. If the doping increases, then the number of polarons reaches a maximum value and begins to pair in bipolarons. This is easily followed by magnetic susceptibility measurements that show an increase with increasing doping to reach a maximum followed by a decrease indicative of spin pairing in bipolarons.16 The second possible mechanism to explain electrical conductivity in conducting polymers is solitons. Solitons are localized conformational defects that can easily move coupled with the lattice vibrations since the energy of the solitons is the same everywhere (in contrast to electrons whose energy varies from site to site).12,17,18 In one-dimensional conjugated systems at low temperatures this mechanism gives rise to an insulating state since solitons are trapped, given the low dimensionality of the lattice. In the case of solitons generated by doping (as in the case of slightly doped conducting polymers) the charge born by the solitons interact with the doping counterions, reducing their mobility. When the numbers of solitons increase (as in highly doped conducting polymers) the solitons form a partially filled band leading to a band-type conductivity. Finally, when charged and neutral solitons coexist, the trapped charged solitons may hop if the neutral solitons pass near the charged ones. This mechanism is called the intersoliton hopping and constitutes a particular case of hopping conductivity.
Other factors that may determine and explain the conductivity in one-dimensional CPs are the localization effects. These effects are very important when dealing with very small wires (molecular wires in some cases) and are attributed to the presence of unavoidable defects in the lattice.19–21 In these cases, the level of impurities determines the localization of the electrons and if the impurities level is high enough, the electrons will only present a hopping mechanism between localized states. This localization explains the trend towards an insulating state in strictly one-dimensional conductors when the temperature tends to zero, as observed in one dimensional CPs, even if they present, in some cases, weak inter-chain interactions.
## 2.3. Electronic transitions in CPs and low dimensional conductors
Although the main conducting mechanisms in doped polymers and CPs are now well established, there are many systems (specially molecular and polymeric) that undergo electronic transitions which may be accompanied by structural ones when the temperature and/or the pressure are changed. These electronic transitions are usually driven by the relatively strong electron–lattice or spin–lattice interactions present in these systems due to the low dimensionality of the lattice. Among these transitions the most frequent are the Peierls and spinPeierls transitions observed in one dimensional systems as KCP $(\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{CN})_{4}\mathrm{Br}_{0.30}3.2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ and TTF–TCNQ (TTF $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ tetrathiafulvalene, TCNQ $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ tetracyanoquinodimethane).22–25 The Peierls transition can be described as a charge density wave (CDW, see below) that opens a gap at the Fermi level giving rise to an insulating state. This CDW is originated by a periodic molecular displacement that breaks the regularity of the chain, resulting in a dimerization of the chain. The driving force for this transition is the gain in electronic energy due to the resulting lowering of the Fermi level.18,26
If the electronic Coulomb repulsion is strong, then the localized spins may suffer a magnetic transition to an antiferromagnetic (AF) ground spin state27,28 or a spin-Peierls (SP) transition giving rise to a diamagnetic dimerized system.29 The AF transition occurs when the system presents nonnegligible inter-chain interactions that increase the electronic dimensionality to two or three. The spin-Peierls transition is observed when the localized spins (that interact along the chain) suffer a spin pairing (i.e., each couple of equally spaced spins in the chain suffers an antiferromagnetic ordering to generate paired spins due to a dimerization of the chain). Here the driving force is the magnetic energy resulting from the antiferromagnetic coupling of the spins in the dimers.
In one dimensional metals the Fermi surface (that in a partially filled band represents the limiting area between the occupied and empty wave vector regions) appears as an open surface with a rectangular shape. As the dimensionality of the metal increases this rectangle bends more and more until it closes to form a cylinder for two-dimensional metals and a sphere for three-dimensional metals. When one side of the Fermi surface can be translated in such a way that it overlaps with the other side, then the Fermi surface is said to be nested. The vector describing this translation is called the nesting vector. In this situation the metallic state is not the stable one30 because the one dimensional metal is expected to suffer a metal insulator transition in the form of a charge density wave (CDW) or a spin density wave (SDW). These two insulating states are obtained due to the mixing of the empty and occupied orbitals near the Fermi surface. This mixing of orbitals is important since the energy difference between them is small and gives rise to modified empty and occupied orbitals. When the temperature is lowered, there are two possibilities: (1) if the originally occupied orbitals are now doubly occupied then the metal will present a CDW. This state is driven by the lattice vibrations (phonons). (2) If the on-site electronic repulsion is strong, then the originally occupied and unoccupied orbitals will bear one electron each (with anti-parallel spins) in order to minimize the on-site electron–electron repulsion. This situation gives rise to a spin density wave (SDW). Both states (CDW and SDW) are insulating because they open a gap at the Fermi level.
Other possible electronic transitions observed in conducting molecular materials and CPs are the Mott insulators (MI), the charge disproportionation (CD) or charge ordering (CO), the Wigner solids (a special case of CO) and the spin-gapped (SG) state (Scheme 4).31
CD or CO appears when the charge distribution along the conducting chain or layer is not uniformly distributed (due to the polarising effect of counterions or to structural dissimilarities, Scheme 4a and d).32–36 The Mott insulators are non-degenerate half-filled band systems where the on-site Coulomb repulsion $(U)$ is stronger than the kinetic energy of the electrons (corresponding to the bandwidth, $W)$ . This situation corresponds to those systems where the number of electrons is equal to the number of sites (no mixed valence exists) and each electron is located in one site to avoid the on-site repulsions, $U$ (Scheme 4b and 4c for two and one dimensional systems, respectively). In the Winger solids besides the on-site Coulomb repulsion $(U)$ , the first neighbour Coulomb repulsion $\mathbf{\xi}^{\mathcal{N}}$ also plays an important role when the kinetic energy of the electrons is low $W\ll U)$ . If these systems present a quarter filled band then the electrons will be localized on alternating sites to minimize both, $U$ and $V$ (Scheme 4a and d). This special case of CO (the charge orders in alternating sites) is called the Winger solid. If these systems present a dimerization then the electron will occupy the bonding molecular orbital formed inside the dimer. This is the so-called dimer-Mott state, where a localized and uniform charge distribution is present along the chain (Scheme 4e). The spin-gapped state is a special case of a Mott localized insulator where the spins not only are localized on adjacent sites but are antiferromagnetically coupled to generate a non-magnetic ground state at low temperatures.
Finally, it is worth to mention that although very scarce, there are CPs that present proton conductivity. This conductivity has been attributed to the presence of water clusters,38 –OH groups in the coordinated ligands39 and even to one dimensional nanoarrays of water molecules,40 although there are no definitive proofs of these mechanisms yet.
## 3. One-dimensional coordination polymers
## 3.1. Halides bridging metal entities
One extensively studied class of quasi-one-dimensional polymers encompass species with the abridged formulation MX and MMX where M is a metal cation, commonly, Ni, Pd or Pt and X is a halogen, like Cl, Br or I, acting as a bridge between sequentially spaced metallic centres. These two akin families are of great interest, not only for their potential electrical transport behaviour, but also for their wide scope of physical properties. Prime examples are luminescence spectra with large Stokes shifts,41 large third-order nonlinear optical properties, gigantic third-order optical nonlinear susceptibility42 or progressive resonance Raman scattering. Along with those, these materials also exhibit the characteristic features of quasi-one-dimensional polymers such as spin density wave (SDW), charge density wave (CDW), solitons, polarons, and bipolarons.
3.1.1. MX chains. The combination of both ${\mathrm{d}}_{z}^{2}$ orbitals coming from the metal centre with the $\mathfrak{p}_{z}$ orbitals of the halogen bridge renders the theoretically infinite 1D structure ascribed for these materials. Over 300 mixed-valence halogen-bridged
MX-chains species have been reported so far by applying this basic principle. The resumed formula of most of them can be schemed as follows: $\mathrm{[ML_{2}][M L_{2}X_{2}]A_{4}}$ (where ${\bf M}={\bf N i}$ , Pd and Pt, $\mathrm{~L~}=$ alkyl diamine, $\mathrm{\DeltaX=Cl}$ , Br, I and $\mathbf{A}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}$ , Br, I, $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{BF}_{4},\mathrm{SO}_{4},$ ). Some heterobimetallic compounds have also been reported in the literature.
According to their valence ordering, two different series of MX complexes are described as depicted in Fig. 1.
3.1.1.1. Pd and Pt MX chains. The Pd and Pt MX chain compounds present a CDW ground state and a lineal structure formed by alternating square planar $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathbf{L}_{2}$ with octahedral $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{{IV}}}\mathbf{L}_{2}\mathbf{X}_{2}$ subunits, rendering infinite $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{IV}}{-}\mathbf{X}{\cdot}\mathbf{\partial}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}$ chains. The halogen is displaced from the midpoint between metal centres as a consequence of large electron–phonon coupling. These interactions may be tuned by a judicious selection of the in-plane ligands, bridging halides, counterions and metal centres. The interchain interactions can also be increased by introducing H-bond nets that give rise to expanded dimensionalities.
Generally, their synthetic procedure encompasses an equimolecular mixture of both single-valence components, i.e. ${{\bf{M}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ and $\mathrm{\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{IV}}}$ complexes or controlled oxidation of solutions containing the ${{\bf{M}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ component.
In a series of papers during the $70\mathrm{^{\circ}s}$ , Interrante and collaborators studied, among other physical properties, the electrical conductivity and its pressure dependence of selected mixed-valence MX chains,43–45 some of them, like Magnus’ green salts derivatives, which may be regarded as MX systems, are out of the scope of this review, but they also conducted the first electrical characterisation of $\mathrm{[Pt}(e n)_{2}][\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}\mathrm{X}_{2}]\mathrm{X}_{4}$ $e n\ =$ ethylenediamine; $\mathrm{~\bf~X~}=\mathrm{~\bf~Cl~}$ , Br, I) derivatives.43 The synthetic methods for these compounds had been previously reported.46–48 At ambient temperature and pressure, the conductivity of these sort of complexes along the chain direction is rather low, $<10^{-8}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ , both in single-crystal and polycrystalline samples. Significantly, their conductivity rapidly increases under pressure, with a fully reversible and reproducible behaviour. A polycrystalline sample of $[\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}][\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}\mathrm{I}_{2}]\mathrm{I}_{4}$ had an electrical conductivity of almost $1\:\mathrm{S}\:\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at 160 kbar whilst that found for $[\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}][\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}\mathbf{B}\mathrm{r}_{2}]\mathbf{B}\mathrm{r}_{4}$ analogue was $10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ , the least conductor species of the series was $\mathrm{[Pt}(e n)_{2}][\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}]\mathrm{Cl}_{4}$ displaying a $10^{-7}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ value. The trend $\mathbf{I}\ >\ \mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}\ >\ \mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}$ could be explained in terms of better metal–halogen orbital overlaps. The authors assumed that pressure played a role in reducing the geometrical inequivalency of the metal environments and increasing the intrachain metal–halogen orbital overlaps. They also studied the electrical conductivity of Wolffram’s red and Reilhlen’s green salts $(\mathrm{[Pt(C_{2}H_{5}N H_{2})_{4}][P t(C_{2}H_{5}N H_{2})_{4}C l_{2}]C l_{4}}$ and $[\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{4}]-$ $\mathrm{[Pt(C_{2}H_{5}N H_{2})_{4}B r_{2}]B r_{4}},$ respectively), but their conductivities were below the limit of detection even above 140 kbar.
On a latter report, Interrante and Browall continued studying $\mathrm{[Pt}(e n)_{2}][\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}\mathrm{X}_{2}]\mathrm{X}_{4}$ systems.43 Four-probe conductivity measurements were conducted on both single crystals and pressed pellets. Electrical contacts were made with 2 mm $\mathcal{O}$ platinum wires for the pellet samples and $0.5\ \mathrm{mm}\ \mathrm{~\textem~}$ gold coated tungsten wires for single crystals and the wires were attached with conducting silver paint. Results of dc conductivity measurements at room temperature were reported as follows: $<10^{-11}$ and $10^{-10}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ for single crystals of $\mathrm{[Pt}(e n)_{2}][\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}]\mathrm{Cl}_{4}$ and $[\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}][\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}]\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{4}.$ , respectively, suitable crystals of the $\mathrm{[Pt}(e n)_{2}][\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}\mathrm{I}_{2}]\mathrm{I}_{4}$ species could not be grown. Values found for pressed pellet samples were $\sim10^{-15}$ , $5\ \times\ 10^{-13}$ and $1\times10^{-9}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{[Pt}(e n)_{2}][\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}]\mathrm{Cl}_{4}.$ , $[\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}][\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}\mathrm{Br}_{2}]\mathrm{Br}_{4}$ and $[\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}][\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}\mathrm{I}_{2}]\mathrm{I}_{4}$ , respectively.
In 1981, the research group of Kida reported the electrical conductivity of several $\mathrm{[ML_{2}][M L_{2}X_{2}]A_{4}}$ MX chains;49 the measurements were carried on single crystals using a two-probe method with silver wires contacted with carbon paste along the needle direction. The complexes $[\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}][\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}\mathrm{I}_{2}](\mathrm{BF}_{4})_{4}$ and $[\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}][\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}\mathrm{X}_{2}](\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{4}$ $\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\boldmath~{~\cal~X~}~}}=\ensuremath{\mathrm{\boldmath~{~\cal~C}~}}_{}$ , Br, I) exhibited electrical conductivity values of $1.6\times10^{-9}$ , $2.0\times10^{-15}$ , $3.0\times10^{-11}$ and $1.8\times10^{-8}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ , respectively (Table 1 and Fig. 2). They also found that larger conductivity values could be observed on single crystal samples.50
Kida et al. also reported that $[\mathrm{Pt}(t n)_{2}][\mathrm{Pt}(t n)_{2}\mathrm{X}_{2}](\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{4}$ 1 $\mathbf{\chi}_{t n}~=~\mathbf{\chi}_{}$ triethylenediamine, $\mathrm{~\boldmath~X~}=\mathrm{~\boldmath~Br~}$ and ${\mathrm{I}})^{49}$ show similar conductivity values $(2.0\times10^{-14}$ and $7\times10^{-7}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ , respectively) to those reported for the en analogues $(3.0\times10^{-11}$ and $1.8\ \times$ $10^{-8}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively, Table 1).49
The slow diffusion of $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ vapour into a methanolic solution of $\mathrm{Pt}(c h n x)_{2}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ afforded single-crystals of $\mathrm{[Pt(}c h n x\mathrm{)_{2}I]I_{2}}$ $\cdot c h n x=$ $1R,2R$ -diaminocyclohexane) as reported by Yamashita and Takaishi in 2006.53 Fig. 3 depicts a view of the solid-state structure evidencing the planar environment of the $[\mathrm{Pt}(c h n x)_{2}]$ moieties which are bridged by half-occupied midpoint-displaced bridging iodine, forming linear chains. The authors studied an anomalous valence state behaviour caused by the fast fluctuation between $\mathrm{Pt}^{\mathrm{II}}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathrm{I}{-}\mathrm{Pt}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ and $\mathrm{Pt}^{\mathrm{IV}_{-}}\mathrm{I}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathrm{Pt}^{\mathrm{II}}$ in the double well potential, by using EPR, X-ray diffuse scattering and STM images and recorded the electrical conductivity of the complex using the four-probe method with carbon paste. The observed value was of the order of $10^{-4}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at room temperature. They also claimed the smallest charge-transfer energy observed for this complex compared with all the other halogen-bridged MX Pt compounds.
3.1.1.2. Ni MX chains. Conversely to $\mathrm{\bfPt}$ and Pd species, due to the strong electron correlation ascribed to Ni metal centres, their charge distribution along a MX compound is best described as infinite $\mathrm{\mathbf{Ni}^{\mathrm{{III}}}{-}\mathbf{X-Ni}^{\mathrm{{III}}}}$ chains. The strong ligand field forces the $\mathrm{Ni}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ion to be in a low spin state $\mathrm{(Ni^{III}d^{7}}$ ; $S=1/2)$ with an unpaired electron occupying the ${\mathrm{d}}_{z}^{2}$ orbital. In this situation, the halide sits on the midpoint of neighbouring Ni pairs conforming a Mott–Hubbard semiconductor with a SDW ground state. Standard synthetic preparations of Ni-based MX chains include previous electrolysis of the divalent discrete compounds in the presence of a pertinent electrolyte. In this way, many $\mathrm{[NiL_{2}][N i L_{2}X_{2}]A_{4}}$ chains have been isolated but only a few selected ones have been the subject of electrical conductivity studies.
In 1981, Kida et al. also characterised single crystals of the compound $[\mathrm{Ni}(e n)_{2}][\mathrm{Ni}(e n)_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}](\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{4}$ .54 The contacts were done with silver wires and carbon paste in a two-probe experiment, the observed electrical conductivity was $1.3\times10^{-8}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$
A new series of 1D Ni MX chains were reported in 1999 by the group of Yamashita,55 both chemical and electrochemical methods were used for the isolation of $[\mathrm{Ni}(c h x n)_{2}][\mathrm{Ni}(c h x n)_{2}\mathrm{X}_{2}]\mathrm{Y}_{4}$ species $\mathrm{(X=Cl}$ , Br and $\mathrm{(Cl/Br)}$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaY=Cl^{-}}$ , $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{(Cl/Br)}$ , ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}{}^{-}$ ), as expected, X-ray crystallography confirmed a planar $\mathrm{N}_{4}$ donor set for the two in-plane diamine ligands with the $\mathrm{Ni}(c h x n)_{2}$ fragments bridged and stacked along the $b$ axis in all cases. The slow diffusion of $\mathbf{Br}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ into 2-methoxypropanol solutions of $[\mathrm{Ni}(c h x n)_{2}]\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ and $[\mathrm{Ni}(c h x n)_{2}]\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}$ led to the isolation of $\mathrm{Ni}(c h x n)_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2.459}\mathrm{Br}_{0.541}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}(c h x n)_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{1.28}\mathrm{Br}_{1.72}$ , respectively, X-ray diffraction proved to be a powerful tool in order to determine the $\mathrm{Cl}$ and Br ratio in both mixedhalide compounds. Although their structures were described previously,56 no electrical characterisation experiments were undertaken at that moment. ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ derivatives were obtained by both chemical and electrochemical methods.
The electrical conductivities of some selected compounds were measured on single-crystals using gold wires and carbon paste with the four-probe method.57 The room temperature conductivities decreased in the order of $\mathrm{Ni}(c h x n)_{2}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{3}\ >$ $\mathrm{Ni}(c h x n)_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{1.28}\mathrm{Br}_{1.72}~>~\mathrm{Ni}(c h x n)_{2}\mathrm{Br}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}~>~\mathrm{Ni}(c h x n)_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{3}|$ and presented the following values: $6.92\times10^{-4}$ , $1.86\times10^{-5}$ , $2.45\times10^{-6}$ and $2.45\times10^{-7}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ , respectively (Table 2). Crystals of $\mathrm{Ni(chxn)_{2}C l_{2.459}B r_{0.541}}$ were too small to be measured.
Fig. 4 shows the electrical conductivity dependence with temperature for both $\mathrm{Ni}(c h x n)_{2}\mathrm{Cl}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}(c h x n)_{2}\mathrm{Br}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ .
Takaishi et al. published in 2008 that the electrolysis of $[\mathrm{Ni}(S,S-b n)_{2}]\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ $\begin{array}{l l l}{(S,S{\ –}b n}&{=}&{2S,3S{\mathrm{-}}}\end{array}$ diaminobutane) in anhydrous 1-propanol under a constant $10~\upmu\mathrm{A}$ current yielded novel $[\mathrm{Ni}(S,S{\ –}b n)_{2}\mathrm{Br}]\mathrm{Br}_{2}\mathrm{MX}$ species using tetramethylammonium bromide as the electrolyte.59 Suitable crystals for $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction studies were grown over a two-week period. The evaluation of its solid-state structure showed a stronger Ni–Br bond than that of the chxn derivative. Thus, in $[\mathrm{Ni}(S,S{\cdot}b n)_{2}\mathrm{Br}]\mathbf{Br}_{2}$ the Ni Ni contact along the 1D chain is 5.129(1) $\mathring\mathbf{A}$ at $120\mathrm{K}$ , whereas in $[\mathrm{Ni}(c h x n)_{2}\mathbf{Br}]\mathbf{Br}_{2}$ this distance is 5.157(1) $\mathring\mathbf{A}$ at the same temperature. The electrical conductivity of the $[\mathrm{Ni}(S,S{\ –}b n)_{2}\mathrm{Br}]\mathbf{Br}_{2}$ complex, measured with the four contact method, showed a quite high room temperature conductivity value of $10^{-1}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , decreasing to almost $2.0\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at ca. $133\mathrm{~K~}$ (Table 2). Since the $\mathbf{X}$ -ray diffraction showed the presence of some ${\bf N}_{1}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ impurities (due to the synthetic procedure), the conductivity may be attributed to these $\mathrm{{Ni}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ impurities.58
3.1.2. Heterobimetallic MX chains. When $[\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}\mathrm{X}_{2}]\mathrm{X}_{2}$ complexes are mixed with $\mathrm{Ni}(e n)_{2}\mathrm{X}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Pd}(e n)_{2}\mathrm{X}_{2}$ species, hetero-metal mixed-valence MX compounds result. Firstly reported by Papavassiliou60,61 and Kida,62 they have been the subject of later electrical conductivity exploration. Thus, Yamashita et al. compiled their electrical properties studied on single crystals in a later report.52,63 Crystals of $\mathrm{Ni(\mathrm{II}){-}X{-}P t(\mathrm{IV})}$ species were grown from aqueous solutions containing $\mathrm{NaClO_{4}}$ whereas those of $\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{II}){\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{X-}\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{IV})$ were obtained from diluted $\mathrm{{HClO}_{4}}$ solutions. Conductivity measurements performed along the needle axis for $\mathrm{[Pd}(e n)_{2}\mathrm{]}[\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}\mathrm{X}_{2}](\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{4}$ showed room temperature values of $4~\times~10^{-18}$ , $1.4~\times~10^{-15}$ and $4.5\times10^{-11}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ , for $\mathrm{~\bf~X~}=\mathrm{~\bf~Cl~}$ , Br and I, respectively. Those for $\mathrm{[Ni}(e n)_{2}][\mathrm{Pt}(e n)_{2}\mathrm{X}_{2}](\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{4}$ were $3.2\times10^{-17}$ and $8.4\times10^{-14}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{\DeltaX=Cl}$ and Br, respectively (Table 3). The experimental data decreased in the order of $\mathbf{I}>\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}>\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}$ in both families and values are higher for Ni derivatives than for Pd. The temperature dependence is shown in Fig. 5.
Recent attention has been focused on a novel non-stoichiometrical hetero-metal $[\mathrm{Ni}_{1-x}\mathrm{Pd}_{x}(c h x n)_{2}\mathrm{X}]\mathrm{X}_{2}$ ( $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{X}\ =\mathbf{\nabla}\mathrm{Cl}\$ , Br) backbone. Yamashita et al. reported in 1999, that combined methanolic solutions containing different ratios of $\mathrm{Ni}(c h x n)_{2}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Pd}(c h x n)_{2}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}$ precursors were electrochemically oxidised with tetramethylammonium halides as electrolytes, after several weeks, good quality single crystals of $[\mathrm{Ni}_{1-x}\mathrm{Pd}_{x}(c h x n)_{2}\mathrm{Br}]\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ were formed on H-shaped cells.57 The Ni–Pd ratio was determined by inductively coupled plasma emission spectrometry, X-ray powder patterns of the $[\mathbf{Ni}_{1-x}\mathbf{Pd}_{x}(c h x n)_{2}\mathbf{Br}]\mathbf{Br}_{2}$ complex revealed an isomorphous phase with the same $I222$ space group of the parent starting materials $\mathrm{Ni}(c h x n)_{2}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Pd}(c h x n)_{2}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ hence a halogen-bridged linear structure was assumed for the mixed-metal Ni–Pd species. Interestingly, the $\mathrm{Pd}^{\mathrm{II}}{-}\mathrm{X}{-}\mathrm{Pd}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ mixed-valence state gradually changed to the unusual $\mathrm{{\bfPd}^{\mathrm{III}}{-}}\mathrm{{\bfX}{-}}\mathrm{{\bfPd}^{\mathrm{III}}}$ state on increasing the ${\mathrm{Ni}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ content. This observation indicates that the electron–phonon interaction in $\mathrm{Pd}^{\mathrm{II}}{-}\mathrm{X-Pd}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ systems is destabilized by strong electron correlation of the ${\bf N i}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ion that promotes the change from a CDW state to a SDW state with a crossover between both at $x\approx0.7$ IR, Raman, XPS and Auger spectrometry corroborated these results. Three years later, in 2002, the same authors informed on the electrical resistance of $[\mathrm{Ni}_{1-x}\mathrm{Pd}_{x}(c h x n)_{2}\mathrm{Br}]\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ and its dependence with increasing pressure, however, no explicit conductivity results were communicated.64
3.1.3. Other related mixed-valence chains. Other mixedvalence chains have been prepared involving different donor atom sets. Thus, the group of Castan explored the coordination modes of a variety of thioureas, thioamides, dithiomalonamides and related ligands combined with Pd and $\mathrm{\bfPt}$ cations during the early 80’s.65–67 In this way, they showed that the dithiomalonamide skeleton (HL) complexes both Pd and $\mathrm{\bfPt}$ to form two different series of mixed-valence species which are best formulated as $\mathrm{[M^{II}(H L)_{2}][M^{I V}X_{6}]}$ and $[\mathrm{M}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{HL})_{2}]\mathrm{-}$ $[\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{IV}}(\mathbf{H}\mathbf{L})_{2}\mathbf{X}_{2}]\mathbf{X}_{4}$ $\mathbf{M}\ =\ \mathbf{Pt}$ and $\mathrm{Pd}$ ; $\mathbf{X}\ =\ \mathbf{Cl}$ , Br and I), as depicted in Fig. 6. Their proposed $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathbf{X}\mathbf{-M}^{\mathrm{IV}}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{X}$ structures are highly reminiscent of that of MX compounds.
The lack of $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction data precluded a concise determination of the solid state structure of all these complexes; however, elemental analyses were consistent with the proposed molecular formulae, while detailed coordination environments were unambiguously resolved by $^{195}\mathrm{Pt}\mathrm{NMR}$ spectroscopy for the $\mathrm{\mathbf{P}\mathrm{t}}$ analogues and inferred for the Pd ones.
Powder conductivity measurements were determined on pressed pellets evidencing the semiconductor nature of the samples (Table 4).
The electrical properties of the complexes depend on the metal centre, on the coordination donor set and on the linking halogen (X). As observed for parent MX chains, the conductivity increases in the sequence $\mathbf{Cl}<\mathbf{Br}<\mathbf{I}$ . Pd derivatives have higher values than the $\mathrm{\mathbf{P}}\mathrm{t}$ ones, and a significant increase, circa two orders of magnitude, is observed on moving from the $\mathrm{[M^{II}(H L)_{2}][M^{I V}X_{6}]}$ series to the $[\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{HI}}(\mathrm{HL})_{2}][\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{IV}}(\mathrm{HL})_{2}\mathbf{X}_{2}]\mathbf{X}_{4}$ one. The authors postulated this trend as a consequence of a better overlap of ${\mathrm{d}}_{z}^{2}$ orbitals which may lead to a $\mathbf{M}(\mathrm{{\mathrm{III}}})$ -like behaviour.
The coordination chemistry of the dibenzyl sulfide ligand towards gold was firstly explored by Herrmann by the turn of the 19th century.68 At that time he isolated an orange precipitate with a $[\mathrm{Au(DBS)Cl_{2}}]$ $\mathrm{(DBS~=~}$ dibenzyl sulfide) formulation. Following this study, Smith reported on the bromide derivative obtained during the study of the addition compounds of gold halides with benzyl sulfide.69 It was in 1952 when the first structural assumption for these complexes was made by Brain and collaborators.70 Their preliminary $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ -ray study suggested the presence of a $\mathsf{1D A u(m a)–C l\cdot\cdot\cdot A u(\mathrm{I})}$ mixedvalence linear chain in the solid state structure, analogous to that observed in MX chains. However, the disordered crystal structure of $[\mathrm{Au(DBS)Cl_{2}}]$ species was not resolved until 1988 by Takahashi and Tanino,71 who evidenced the infinite $\operatorname{Au}(\operatorname{III}){\operatorname{-Cl}}\cdot\cdot\cdot\operatorname{Au}(\operatorname{I})$ chain with a positional disorder for the bridging chlorine atom, a common feature observed in MX chains (Fig. 7). Another remarkable characteristic of the structure is the pairing of two 1D chains, the shortest interchain contact being $3.32\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , shorter than the average intrachain $\mathrm{\Au\mathrm{-}C l}$ (bridging) distance of $3.41\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ . The structure of the isomorphous $[\mathrm{Au(DBS)Br_{2}}]$ derivative was also reported by the same group three years later (Fig. 7).72 The electrical conductivities of both chain compounds were measured by Interrante and Bundy in 1977,45 as for most MX chains, these complexes are described as poor conductors at room temperature with $\sigma<10^{-10}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ but their conductivities increase when applying pressure, reaching a broad maximum at ca. 250 kbar with recorded conductivities of $10^{-6}$ and $10^{-3}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ for the chlorine and bromine derivatives, respectively. The measurements were performed using the four-probe method with $25~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}~\emptyset$ gold wires and silver paint. Measurements under pressure were carried out on a Drickamer apparatus with diamond compact-tipped pistons.45
An important consequence of passing from MX to MMX chains is the gain of internal degrees of freedom from the charge–spin–lattice point of view. Thus, now there are new valence ordered states different from those inherent to MX compounds. These states can be summarized as average valence (AV), charge polarisation (CP), charge density wave (CDW) and alternate-charge polarisation (ACP) states (Scheme 5).
As previously stated, some of the MX compounds are best described as CDW systems, in particular those containing Pd or Pt, whereas those with Ni are characterised as being Mott–Hubbard semiconductors. The pop MMX’s are reported to have a CDW ground state (closely related to MX) while SCS have an ACP metal-sublattice dimerisation at low temperature. Nonetheless, fine tuning of ground state transitions can be achieved applying pressure or by replacing halogen bridges, counterions and ligands. In most cases, the nature of the metallic phases of these chains is attributable to AV or CP electronic structure stabilisation.
Two main routes can be followed in order to isolate the desired mixed-valence species, the first method consist in co-crystallising equimolar solutions of the MM and $\mathbf{MMX}_{2}$ precursors. A second procedure implies partial oxidation through halogen addition. In certain cases, as for pop systems, a precipitating agent is also combined in the reaction mixtures. Both dta $\displaystyle(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CS}_{2}^{-})$ and pop derivatives give rise to 1D chains; the dta derivatives are neutral with ligand helical arrangements wrapping the bimetallic core, while the diphosphite derivatives are negatively charged linear chains with almost no twist and the need of a counter-ion.
3.1.4.1. Dithioacids with platinum. The first report of the electrical properties of $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ , published by C. Bellitto et al. in 1983, started the quest for metallic conduction involving dta MMX. At room temperature, a compressed pellet of $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ showed a maximum electrical conductivity of $7\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ .73 Yamashita et al. reported in 1989 that the electrical conductivity performed on single crystals with the two contact method was as high as $2\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at atmospheric pressure and $10\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ at $7\mathrm{GPa}$ . This increase with applied pressure is probably due to an increase in the energy transfer or a decrease in the band gap and Peirls distortion as pressure rises up.74 They also claimed that thin films of the material could be prepared in vacuum with a conductivity of $0.2\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ at room temperature.74 Finally, in 1997, Kitagawa and co-workers provided a new value of $13\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ ,75 reinforced by another report of the same group in 1999.76 This discrepancy in the reported values reflects the already mentioned fact that the exact value of the conductivity may be affected by the quality and purity of the single crystals (and by the accuracy in the determination of the dimensions of the crystals). The dc electrical conductivity measurements were carried out along the crystallographic $b$ axis (i.e. through the $\mathrm{\mathbf{P}t\mathrm{-}\mathbf{P}t}$ chain using a standard four-probe technique with gold paint and $20~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ + gold wires, between 50 and $380\mathrm{~K~}$ . Metallic behaviour was recorded in the $300{-}340\ \mathrm{K}$ range.
Increasing the number of carbon atoms in the lateral chain of the dithiocarboxylic acid may tune the observed $\mathrm{\mathbf{P}t\mathrm{-}\mathbf{P}t}$ and $\mathrm{Pt\mathrm{-}I}$ distances. It can be anticipated that bulkier aliphatic chains will impose a demanding steric repulsive interaction resulting in elongated $\mathrm{\mathbf{P}}\mathrm{t}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{\mathbf{P}}\mathrm{t}$ contacts. In 2001, Mitsumi et al. reported the synthesis and characterisation of novel $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(\mathrm{EtCS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ species.77 Single crystal X-ray analyses confirmed the 1D nature of the structure. The $\mathrm{Pt{-}P t}\stackrel{\cdot}{(2.684(1)}\stackrel{\circ}{\mathrm{A}})$ and $\mathrm{Pt\mathrm{-}I}$ (average $2.980\mathring{\mathrm{A}}_{\varepsilon}^{\cdot}$ ) distances measured at $293\mathrm{~K~}$ were slightly longer than those observed for the parent $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ compound $(\mathrm{Pt-Pt}2.677(2)\mathring{\mathbf{A}},$ Pt–I (average) $2.978\mathring\mathrm{A}_{\/,}$ . Electrical transport measurements concluded that the polymer was a metallic conductor showing an electrical conductivity between 5 and $30\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ at room temperature (again the values differ for different crystals). The results were tested in several single crystals using the four-probe technique with gold paint contacts and $25~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}~\emptyset$ gold wires. The temperature was varied from 80 to $400\mathrm{~K~}$ and a metallic-semiconductor transition was observed at 205 K.77
Several MMX chains were reported later in the following year by the same authors.78,79 In these compounds the dimetallic $\mathrm{\mathbf{P}t\mathrm{-}\mathbf{P}t}$ units were built with $\scriptstyle n\mathrm{-PrCS}_{2}\mathrm{H}$ and $n{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{BuCS}_{2}\mathrm{H}$ dithioacid ligands to form the corresponding $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(n{\cdot}\mathrm{Pr}\mathrm{CS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ and $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(n{-}\mathrm{BuCS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ chain polymers. These compounds were examined with classical inorganic techniques and further characterised through four-probe electrical transport measurement on single crystals. The $\boldsymbol{n}\mathrm{-BuCS}_{2}$ derivative displayed at room temperature an electrical conductivity in the range $17{-}83\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ and a phase transition at $325\mathrm{~K~}$ . The same year Nakazawa and co-workers published a heat capacity study of the complex and reported an approximate electrical conductivity of $30\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ (in the range already observed by Mitsumi and Toriumi) with weak temperature dependence.80 $\mathrm{~In~}~2002$ , Mitsumi et al. reported that $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(n{\cdot}\mathrm{Pr}\mathrm{CS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ showed an electrical conductivity value of ca. $0.23~\mathrm{S~cm}^{-1}$ at room temperature, a value similar to those of other dta family members.79
On a later report concerning the heat capacity of $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(n{\cdot}\mathrm{PenCS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ , published in 2005, Saito’s group reported a room temperature conductivity value of $0.84~\mathrm{S~cm}^{-1}$ with a metal–insulator transition at $235\mathrm{~K~}$ .81
The obvious difference between these MMX derivatives is the alkyl chain length, which governs the interchain distances, responsible for the observed physical properties. These properties are, at the same time, intimately related to the valence-ordered state. The RT metallic phases of both $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(\mathrm{MeCS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ and $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(\mathrm{EtCS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ are associated with AV states, the crystal structure of $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(n{\cdot}\mathrm{Pr}\mathrm{CS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ at RT showed three well differentiated $\mathrm{Pt\mathrm{-}I}$ distances as well as alternatively disordered dta ligands. When temperature increases, a phase transition occurs at $359\mathrm{~K~}$ , giving rise to a pure AV state for the chain. Similar features are observed for $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(n{-}\mathbf{Bu}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{S}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ .
DC electrical conductivity measurements carried out on crystals of $[\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(n{\cdot}\mathrm{pentyl}\mathrm{CS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}]$ showed room temperature conductivities in the range $0.3{-}1.4\ \mathrm{S}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ .82 Two different experiments were used for the conductivity measurements.
In the second experiment, the crystals were heated from 300 to $400\mathrm{K}$ and then cooled to $100\mathrm{K}$ , followed by a final heating scan from 100 to $400\mathrm{~K~}$ (Fig. 8b). An irreversible RT-HT transition was confirmed. The heated crystals also show an abrupt metal–insulator transition at ca. $200~\mathrm{K}$ and after this M–I transition, the crystals again suffered important microcracks and showed a resistivity $10^{3}–10^{4}$ times higher than that observed before the transition. The structural changes that cause the electrical behaviour observed for $[\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(n{\cdot}\mathrm{pentyl}\mathrm{CS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}]$ were confirmed by X-ray diffraction analyses (Fig. 8). Studies on crystals of $[\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(n{\cdot}\mathrm{pentyl}\mathrm{CS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}]$ carried out at three temperatures (100 (LT), 298 (RT) and 350 (HT) K) showed the existence of three different phases for $[\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(n{\cdot}\mathrm{pentyl}\mathrm{CS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}]$ .
The last MMX chain incorporating dta ligands came out in 2009.83 The group of Saito conducted a study concerning the heat capacity, absorption spectrum, spin susceptibility and electrical conductivity of $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(n{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Hex}\mathrm{CS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ . The four-probe method revealed a value of $2\times10^{-3}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ at room temperature, a value slightly lower than those typically observed for $\mathrm{\mathbf{P}\mathrm{t}}$ MMX derivatives (Table 5).
The data collected in Table 5 summarized the excellent electrical conductive properties found in these polymers.
3.1.4.2. Dithioacids with nickel. In addition to $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ , Bellitto et al. also reported the synthetic procedure, isolation and characterisation of $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ back in 1985.84 Its solid-state structure consisted of linear $\mathrm{Ni_{2}(C H_{3}C S_{2})_{4}\mathrm{-I\mathrm{-Ni_{2}(C H_{3}C S_{2})_{4}\mathrm{-I\mathrm{-}}}}}$ chains stacked along the $b$ crystallographic axis. They also measured an electrical conductivity of $5\times10^{-6}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ for what at that time was the first example of a conductive nickel mixedvalence 1D-chain. The electrical measurements were made on pressed pellets of the compound using the two-probe technique. Later electrical conductivity measurements performed by Yamashita’s group on single crystals with the four-probe method gave an improved conductivity of 2.5  102 S cm1.85
Both $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{MeCS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{EtCS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ derivatives present a CP state at room temperature. In the $n$ -Pr derivative, $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(n{\cdot}\mathrm{Pr}\mathrm{CS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ , there are three significantly different Ni–I distances, the valence order can be regarded as –I Ni3+–Ni2+–I Ni2.5+–Ni2.5+– $\mathrm{I\cdot\cdot\cdotNi^{2+}{-N i^{3+}{-I\cdot\cdot\cdot}}}$ , rendering an approximate AV ground state at RT, attributed to the entropy reservoir of the ligand alkyl chains. A similar behaviour was observed in $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(n{\cdot}\mathrm{BuCS}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{I}$ chains. The four $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(d t a)_{4}\mathrm{I}$ compounds show semiconducting behaviour as well as an intense sharp absorption band at $5400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which is assigned to a Mott-Hubbard gap due to a relatively large on-site Coulomb repulsion energy $(U)$ of the nickel atoms.
3.1.4.3. Platinum with pop. The $\mathrm{A}_{4}[\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(p o p)_{4}\mathrm{X}]{\cdot}n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ 0 $\mathbf{A}\ =$ alkali metal, ammonium, alkyl ammonium and alkyl diammonium; $p o p={\bf P}_{2}{\bf O}_{5}{\bf H}_{2}^{2-},$ ; $n=0,2$ and 3; and $\mathrm{\DeltaX=Cl}$ , Br, I) family contains more than 50 structurally characterised linear chains, all of them described as being insulators and presenting AV, CP or CDW valence states, in contrast with the dta family which, in some cases, can stabilise an ACP phase related to their metallic electrical transport properties.
In 1983, the group of Gray prepared the first example of partially oxidised $\mathrm{K}_{4}[\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(p o p)_{4}\mathrm{X}]$ linear chains88 and reported the synthesis of $\mathrm{K}_{4}[\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(p o p)_{4}\mathrm{Cl}]$ , $\mathrm{K_{4}[P t_{2}}(p o p)_{4}\mathrm{Br]\cdot3H_{2}O}$ and $\mathrm{K}_{4}[\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(p o p)_{4}\mathrm{I}].$ , the X-ray single-crystal structure of the bromine derivative, $\mathrm{K}_{4}[\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(p o p)_{4}\mathrm{Br}]{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and the electrical properties of the bromine and iodine derivatives, $\mathrm{K}_{4}[\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(p o p)_{4}\mathrm{Br}]{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{K}_{4}[\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(p o p)_{4}\mathrm{I}]$ (Fig. 10). Conductivity measurements carried out on several single crystals of $\mathrm{K_{4}[P t_{2}}(p o p)_{4}\mathrm{Br]}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with the standard two-probe method revealed at room temperature conductivity values in the range $5\times10^{-4}{\mathrm{-}}1\times10^{-3}{\mathrm{~S~cm}}^{-1}$ . The small crystal size of the iodine derivative prevented a detailed examination of the solid-state structure and subsequent measurement of its electrical behaviour; however a compressed pellet showed a value of $2.8\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ . In 1986, Clark and co-workers reported the crystal structure of the chlorine derivative, $\mathrm{K_{4}}[\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(p o p)_{4}\mathrm{Cl}]$ , and showed that it is constituted by $\mathrm{Pt}^{2+}{-}\mathrm{Pt}^{3+}{-}\mathrm{Cl}$ polarised repeating units.
The data collected in Table 7 show the significant (3–5 orders of magnitude) decrease in conductivity between $\mathrm{K}_{4}[\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(p o p)_{4}\mathrm{X}]$ and their $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}(d t a)_{4}\mathrm{I}$ analogues (Table 5).
3.1.4.4. Other MMX systems. Whilst not formally considered as MMX in the literature, there exist a large number of compounds having this formulation. Kawamura et al. reported the first examples of halide-bridged paddle-wheel chains enclosing a $\mathrm{Rh}_{2}^{5+}$ core with wrapping acetamide $(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CONH}_{2}=a c a m)$ ligands in 2000.89
Crystals of $[\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}\mathrm{Cll}_{n}{\cdot}7n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $[\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}\mathrm{Br}]_{n}$ were isolated by reacting the $\mathrm{Rh}_{2}^{5+}$ precursor with sodium halides. Their structures comprise zig-zag chains of repeating $(-\mathbf{R}\mathbf{h}{-}\mathbf{R}\mathbf{h}{-}\mathbf{X}{-})_{n}$ units assisted via hydrogen-bond formation between amidato-NH donors and amidato-O acceptors of adjacent units for the bromide chain and between amidato-O and interstitial water molecules for the chloride derivative as depicted in Fig. 11. Pressed pellets of both compounds showed a conductivity value below $2\times10^{-7}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ at room temperature. One year after, on a subsequent report, the iodide derivative (Fig. 12) was isolated and crystallographically and electrically characterised.90 This derivative was found to be almost isostructural with the dehydrated chlorine chain, crystallising in the same $C2/c$ space group with similar cell parameters. The observed conductivity of a pressed pellet was $\mathrm{\dot{1}0^{-7}S c m^{-1}}$ (Table 8).
Although the halide bridging two paddle-wheel complex has proven to be a successful strategy, it is not the only one. Thus, another approach for the formation of linear chains may include additional metal halide cores as linkers. In this regard, Ebihara et al. have recently published the compounds formed by reaction of $\mathrm{K_{2}M C l_{4}(M=P d,P t)}$ with the cationic complex $\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}^{~+}$ .91 Thus, the reaction between $[\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\cdot$ $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{4}$ yielded the chain compound formulated as $[\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}][\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}\{(\upmu-\mathrm{Cl})_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}\}]\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ , whereas when ${\mathrm{K}}_{2}{\mathrm{PdCl}}_{4}$ was used, two different compounds, formulated as $[\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}][\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}\{(\upmu-\mathrm{Cl})_{2}\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}\}]\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{[Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{2}][\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}\{(\upmu-\mathrm{Cl})_{2}\mathrm{PdCl}(H_{2}O)\}]\cdot H_{2}O,$ were obtained (Fig. 13). All of them present infinite $(\mathrm{-Rh{\mathrm{-Rh}}{\mathrm{-Cl{\mathrm{-M}}{\mathrm{-Cl{-}}}}}})_{n}$ repeating units, forming zig-zag chains in the two first cases and helical in the third one (Fig. 13).
The observed room temperature conductivity values measured on pressed pellets of the two derivatives $[\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\cdot$ $\mathrm{[Rh_{2}(}a c a m)_{4}\{(}\upmu\mathrm{-Cl)_{2}M C l_{2}\}\}.2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(\bf{M}\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~=~}~}\bf{P t}$ and Pd) were around $10^{-7}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ . These values are similar to those observed for related $\mathrm{Rh}_{2}^{5+}$ chains.
Besides these 1D structures, paddle-wheel complexes constructed with $\mathrm{Rh}_{2}^{5+}$ cores and acetamide ligands may also display several coordination modes when bridged by halides, rendering 2D and 3D dimensional structures. These will be discussed in the following sections.
Closely related to MMX polymers, there exists a short number of extended linear metal atom chains bridged by halogen linkers $[\mathbf{M}_{n}\mathbf{X}]_{\infty}$ , however, limited information is reported in terms of their electrical transport properties. A good example of this series was reported in 2005 by Matsumoto’s group, who succeeded in the isolation of a mixed-metal mixed-valence quasi 1D system shown in Fig. 14. Its solid-state structure revealed a $\mathrm{[Pt_{6}R h_{2}]^{19+}}$ repeating octameric core bridged by twocoordinated chloride ions rendering a $[\mathrm{-Pt-Rh{\mathrm{-Pt}}_{4}\mathrm{-Rh{\mathrm{-Pt}}\mathrm{-Cl-}}}]_{n}$ infinite chain.92,94 The octamer can be viewed as being formed by four dimeric units: two inner $\mathrm{\mathbf{P}t\mathrm{-}\mathbf{P}t}$ dimers $({\mathrm{Ptl}}{\mathrm{-Pt}}2$ and $\mathrm{Pt}3\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Pt}4)$ and two outer $\mathrm{Pt-Rh}$ dimers (Pt5–Rh1 and Pt6–Rh2), all of them doubly bridged by pivalamidate ligands that warrant both close metal–metal contacts and interdimeric hydrogen bond formation between amide oxygen and amine groups along the chain. Since two ammonia groups complete the coordination requirements of the Pt cations, two monodentate chloride ligands complete the Rh1 and Rh2 environments and balance the charge.
The electrical conductivity measured by the four-contacts method shows a semiconducting behaviour with a room temperature conductivity of $3.52\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ along the perpendicular chain direction, crystal thin plate morphology precluded conductivity measurements along the chain direction.
During their investigation on $\mathrm{Te}_{2}\mathrm{Rh}_{4}$ cluster formation, Shieh et al. succeeded in the isolation and characterisation of a number of discrete Te–Ru–Cu carbonyl complexes, including the first $\mathrm{{Cu}(B r)\mathrm{{CuCl}}}.$ -bridged $\mathrm{Te}_{2}\mathrm{Rh}_{4}$ octahedral cluster chain polymer: $\mathrm{\{[PPh_{4}]_{2}T e_{2}R u_{4}(C O)_{10}(C u_{4}B r_{2}C l_{2})\cdot T H F\}_{\infty}}$ . The electrical conductivity measurements of this compound showed a room temperature conductivity of $1{-}5\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ and a semiconducting behaviour.93
This polymeric $\{\mathrm{[PPh_{4}]_{2}T e_{2}R u_{4}(C O)_{10}C u_{4}B r_{2}C l_{2}){\cdot}T H F}\}_{\propto}$ chain was prepared by reaction between $\ensuremath{\mathrm{K}_{2}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{TeO}_{3}}$ and $\mathrm{Ru}_{3}(\mathrm{CO})_{12}$ followed by treatment with $\mathrm{PPh}_{4}\mathrm{Br}$ and $\mathrm{{CuCl}}$ . Its crystal structure (Fig. 15) can be described as formed by $\mathrm{Te}_{2}\mathrm{Ru}_{4}$ octahedral units, each Ru centre is coordinated to two terminal CO groups, two $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ cores and a bridging CO group connecting opposite pairs of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ru}}_{2}$ units. There is also a short interaction of one $\mathrm{cu}$ centre with an apical Te atom. These repetitive units are further connected by chloride bridges forming an anionic chain, whose charge is balanced with $\mathrm{PPh}_{4}^{+}$ counterions running along the $b$ axis. A solvated THF molecule is enclosed in the asymmetric unit cell.
## 3.2. Cyanopyridines as linkers
Cyanopyridine ligands offer two different donor nitrogen sites located at their ends. These nitrogen donor atoms differ in steric requirements and basic character. Thus the cyano nitrogen atom is a weaker donor than the pyridine one. Indeed this is the reason why organic molecules, and counteranions can connect to the metal centre easier than the cyano nitrogen, resulting in the formation of complexes in which the $n$ -cyanopyridines act as monodentate ligands and the cyano groups are not coordinated to the metal.
In a work carried out by Henderson and co-workers,95 several one dimensional coordination polymers of $n$ -cyanopyridines $(n=3,4)$ and pyridine-3,4-dicarbonitrile with $\mathrm{{Ag}(I)}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega)}$ have been explored. In some of the polymers reported $n$ -cyanopyridine and pyridine-3,4-dicarbonitrile ligands coordinated in a monodentate fashion while ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{SCN^{-}}$ bridge the metal centres (Fig. 16).
The electrical characterisation carried out with the two-probe method on pressed pellets (Table 9) shows that the coordination polymers [Cu(4-cyanopyridine) $_{2}(\mathrm{SCN})]_{n}$ and $[\mathrm{Ag}(3$ -cyanopyridine) ${}_{2}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})]_{n}$ are insulators in the whole range of temperature $(290-350~\mathrm{K})$ .
[Ag(pyridine-3,4-dicarbonitrile) $)_{2}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})]_{n}$ and $[\mathrm{Cu}$ (pyridine3,4-dicarbonitrile) $\phantom{}_{2}(\mathrm{SCN})]_{n}$ show a semiconductor behaviour although with a different thermal reversibility. Thus, on one hand, the $\mathrm{Ag(\mathrm{I})}$ polymer shows a thermal reversible behaviour and its conductivity increases by a factor of 2 (from $7.37\times10^{-6}$ to $15.1\ \times\ 10^{-6}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}})$ as the temperature increases from $298\mathrm{~K~}$ to $358\mathrm{~K~}$ . On the other hand, the $\mathrm{{Cu(\mathrm{{I})}}}$ polymer shows an irreversible thermal dependence and the conductivity increases only by $25\%$ (from $4.29\times10^{-5}$ to $5.39\times10^{-5}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ when the temperature is varied in the same range (298–348 K). This difference can be explained according to their structures. Thus, in [Ag(pyridine-3,4-dicarbonitrile) ${}_{2}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})]_{n}$ the one dimensional chain shows short and stable $\operatorname{Ag-O}$ bonds that form a stable and effective conjugated system in the measured temperature range. The authors explain the difference claiming that in [Cu(pyridine-3,4-dicarbonitrile) ${\mathsf{\Omega}}_{2}({\mathsf{S C N}})]_{n}$ , the $\mathrm{{Cu(\mathrm{{I})}}}$ in the crystal is reduced at high temperature by the $\mathbf{SCN}^{-}$ resulting in ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{0}$ doped semiconducting behaviour. It should be also mentioned that none of the two polymers shows metal–metal bonding in their structures.
The 1D-coordination polymer $[\{\mathrm{Cu(TANC)}\}]_{n}._{0.5}]_{n}$ was obtained by reaction of 5,6,11,12-tetraazanaphthacene (TANC) and $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]B F_{4}}$ in $\mathbf{MeOH/MeCN}$ . The presence of half an equivalent of non-stoichiometric $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ ions was determined by $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy and quantitative chemical analysis of $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ ions. These $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ ions are generated by the dissociation of $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ anions in the reaction mixture (Fig. 17).96
Conductivity measurements for this complex, performed with the four-probe method, showed a high room temperature conductivity of $50~\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ along the $a$ axis. This value decreases rapidly to $10^{-6}~\mathrm{S}~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $10\mathrm{~K~}$ showing a semiconducting behaviour. Conductivity measurements performed in different crystal directions showed a high anisotropy with room temperature values of 50, 0.91 and 6.90  103 S cm1 along the $a,b$ and $c$ directions, respectively. These results are explained based on the disordered $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ ions, which affect the stacking of the TANC columns since the particular crystal structure of this compound shows alternating layers of $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ ions that disturb the conductivity through the $c$ axis. This means that the crystal behaves as a quasi-1D molecular semiconductor with a relatively good conductivity in the ab plane and a maximum conductivity along the $a$ axis, corresponding to the 1D stacking of the TANC ligands.96 Table 9 collects conductivity data reported on these coordination polymers.
## 3.3. Systems containing nucleobases
The design and construction of coordination polymers containing nucleobases as either terminal or bridging ligands is of interest due to their abilities to act as functional materials given their particular electrical, magnetic, catalytic and nonlinear optical properties. Three-dimensional coordination polymers may also show applications in molecular storage (gases, anionic exchange, etc.) and/or molecular separation. The formation of polymers based on biologically relevant ligands, such as nucleobases, may provide additional applications in biological fields. For instance, some of these structures could be used as models of specific forms of DNA (e.g. M-DNA, $\mathbf{G}_{4},\hdots...)$ .97 It is surprising that there are relatively little number of polymeric structures based only on bridging nucleobases, despite the number of available coordination positions. Recently, one of these examples has attracted particular attention since its structure resembles that initially suggested for M-DNA (a novel type of DNA).98 The initial studies were carried out on the $\mathrm{[Cd(6\mathrm{-}M P)}_{2}\mathrm{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}]_{n}$ $\left(6\mathbf{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{P}\right.=\mathbf{\Phi}6$ -mercaptopurinato) polymer showing an insulating behaviour which could be explained as a consequence of the large HOMO–LUMO band gap.99 Subsequent DFT calculations performed with the aim of designing suitable electrical conductive $[\mathbf{M}(6-\mathbf{MP})_{2}]_{n}$ $\mathbf{M_{\lambda}}=\mathbf{\lambda}$ transition metal, $6{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{P}\ =\ 6$ -mercaptopurinato) onedimensional coordination polymers pointed out that several metal ions such as $\mathrm{{Ni}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ could provide suitable materials.100
Thus, direct hydrothermal reactions between 6-mercaptopurine (6-MPH) and the analogous 6-thioguanine (6-ThioGH) with $\mathrm{NiSO}_{4}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ gave $\mathrm{[Ni(6–MP)_{2}.2H_{2}O]_{\it n}}$ and $\mathrm{[Ni(6\mathrm{-}T h i o G)_{2}\cdot2H_{2}O]_{\it n}}$ (Fig. 18).
The structures of these coordination polymers are rather similar to that with $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ and consist of one-dimensional chains in which the deprotonated nucleobases act as the bridging ligands connecting the metal ions with short $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{M}$ distances (3.677 and $3.64\bar{6}\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . Two probe DC electrical conductivity measurements at $300~\mathrm{K}$ show ohmic behaviour, with conductivity values of $1.10\times10^{-5}$ and $1.34\times10^{-4}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ , for $[\mathrm{Ni}(6\mathbf{-}\mathbf{MP})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}]_{n}$ and $\mathrm{[Ni(6–ThioG)_{2}.2H_{2}O]}_{n}.$ , respectively (Table 10).101 The small differences found in these measurements were rationalized with the help of additional DFT calculations. The main difference is that the lattice constant obtained by GGA-DFT calculations is $2.2\%$ smaller than that obtained by $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray analysis. This leads to an increase of $\sim14\%$ in the HOMO–LUMO gap $(0.77~\mathrm{~eV}$ for the X-ray structure) with respect to the value obtained for the theoretical geometry. The calculated electronic structure of $\mathrm{[Ni(6–ThioG)_{2}.2H_{2}O]}_{n}$ presents similar electronic properties to those observed for $\mathrm{[Ni(6–MP)}_{2}.2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}]_{n}$ , with a calculated HOMO–LUMO gap of $0.69\ \mathrm{{\eV}}$ . By comparing the band structures of $\mathrm{[Ni(6–MP)_{2}.2H_{2}O]_{\it n}}$ and $\mathrm{[Ni(6–ThioG)_{2}.2H_{2}O]}_{n}.$ it is observed that the amino group, present in the $\mathrm{[Ni(6–ThioG)_{2}.2H_{2}O]}_{n}$ mainly introduces modifications in the deep level states lying at about $-1.25\ \mathrm{eV}$ from the Fermi level, and below. The lower gap observed for $\mathrm{[Ni(6–ThioG)_{2}.2H_{2}O]_{\it n}},$ when compared with $\mathrm{\Delta[Ni(6-MP)_{2}.2H_{2}O]_{\it n},}$ is due to the fact that the former presents a smaller lattice constant $(3.65\ \mathring\mathrm{A})$ than the latter $(3.68\mathring\mathrm{A})$ . By using the value we obtained for the difference in band gaps between the two coordination polymers, we have estimated that polymer $\mathrm{[Ni(6–ThioG)_{2}.2H_{2}O]}_{n}$ should have about 20 times more thermally activated intrinsic carriers, at room temperature, than $\left[\mathrm{Ni}(6{\cdot}\mathbf{MP})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\right]_{n}$ which is consistent with the experimental conductivity measurements.
Therefore, it seems that theoretical calculations are a suitable tool to understand experimental measurements but, even more important, to design coordination polymers with the desired property.
## 3.4. Sulfur ligands as linkers
The field of molecular organic conductors has been widely studied in recent years, focusing mainly on the synthesis of charge transfer complexes based on tetrathiafulvalene (TTF).102 Compounds of this type with intermolecular S  S contacts have shown metallic conductivity and, in some few cases, even superconductivity.103 These results have encouraged researchers to select ligands with similar structural features (Fig. 19) and extend the electrical conductivity studies towards coordination compounds and coordination polymers.
TTF derivatives are $\pi$ -electroactive compounds with chelating sites such as the organylthio groups, which could potentially further enhance the bonding and electronic interaction between the metal centre and the organic ligands. These organosulfur groups can act as linkers between metallic centres to form charge transfer complexes that could act as Synth. Met. or semiconductors. The physical properties of these compounds not only depend on the specific molecular properties of the individual components, metal ions and sulfur ligands, but are also strongly influenced by the arrangement of interactions within the crystal lattice.104
The coordination of TTF derivatives to metal ions provides a wide variety of conductive complexes and coordination polymers. These CPs exhibit a broad panel of electrical properties due to the existence of a coordination polymeric network that can be achieved through the coordination of metal ions and/or through the S S contacts. In the following lines we will describe some examples of electrical conductive coordination polymers with this kind of ligands.
A series of heterobimetallic compounds with maleonitriledithiolate $(m n t^{2-})$ ,105 isomaleonitriledithiolate $(i{-}m n t^{2-})$ ,105
4,5-dimercapto-1,3-dithiole-2-thione $(d m i t^{2-})^{106}$ and 1-ethoxycarbonyl-1-cyanoethylene-2,2-dithiolate $(e c d a^{2-})^{107,108}$ have been prepared by reaction of $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{M}^{\prime}\mathbf{L}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{~L~}=\ m n t^{2-}$ , $\scriptstyle{i-m n t^{2-}}$ ecda2, $d m i t^{2}$ ; $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Na}$ and K, and $\mathbf{M}^{\prime}=\mathrm{Cu(II)}$ , Ni(II), $\mathrm{Au(I)})$ with a solution of different metallic salts $(\mathrm{Ag(\mathrm{I})},\mathrm{Pd(\mathrm{II})},\mathrm{Cd(\mathrm{II})}.$ Pb(II), Hg(II), $\mathrm{{Cu(\mathrm{{I})}}}$ and $\mathrm{Au(I)})$ in the appropriate molar ratio. Their structural characterisation have been carried out by spectroscopic techniques suggesting the formation of onedimensional coordination polymers in which the metal entities are connected by the sulfur atoms of the organic ligands to generate heterobimetallic layered polymeric structures (Fig. 20). These structures extend the electron delocalization beyond the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{S}}_{2}$ group and hence exhibit interesting conductivity in its complexes (Table 11).
The electrical characterisation showed that the conductivity of these materials increases upon heating and decreases upon cooling showing a typical semiconductor behaviour (Table 11).
In the case of $[\mathbf{M^{\prime}}_{2}\mathbf{Ni}(d m i t)_{2}]_{n}$ the polymeric structures present additional metal ions that interconnect the $[\mathbf{M}^{\prime}\mathbf{Ni}(d m i t)_{2}]$ $\mathbf{\langleM^{\prime}\rangle}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ , Ag and Au) linear chains through S–M–S bonds (Fig. 21).106 The high value of $1.6~\mathrm{S~cm}^{-1}$ observed for the complex $\mathrm{[Au}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}(d m i t)_{2}]_{n}$ (Table 11) is remarkable. The authors attributed this value to the oxidation state observed in the [Ni(dmit)2] units.106
The iodine doping of the $[\mathrm{MCu}(i-m i t)_{2}]$ derivatives $(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{\mathrm{I}}$ , $\mathrm{Cd}^{\mathrm{II}}$ , $\mathrm{Hg}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and $\mathrm{Pb}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ) seems to be an effective strategy for the synthesis of electrically conductive compounds. A remarkable increase in the conductivity, i.e. $10^{-6}{-}10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ for the iodine-doped coordination polymers as compared to $10^{-9}{-}10^{-6}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ for the respective undoped compounds, is observed.105 The authors state that this could be due to stronger intermolecular $\mathbf{S\cdot\cdot}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{S}$ stack formation as well as partial oxidation– reduction (mixed valence state) of the metal ions.
In a similar approach, N. Singh and Prasad have synthesized several heterobimetallic coordination polymers (1D and 2D) by reaction of two equivalents of the corresponding mixed metal salts with one equivalent of benzothiazole-2-thiolate $(b z t a^{-})$ or diethylthiocarbamate $(E t_{2}d t c^{-})$ and one equivalent of chalcogenocyanates $(\mathrm{XCN^{-}})$ $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}=\mathbf{\boldsymbol{S}} $ or Se).113 In this case, the chelating-bridging characteristics of the $\mathrm{Et}_{2}\mathrm{dtc}^{-}$ ligand and the hetero-donor atoms ligands $(b z t a^{-}$ and $\mathrm{XCN^{-}},$ ) yield mixed ligand heterobimetallic 1D polymers as is shown in Fig. 22.
$\scriptstyle m n t^{2-}=$ maleonitriledithiolate, $\scriptstyle{i-m n t^{2-}}\ =$ isomaleonitriledithiolate, $d m i t^{2-}=4{,}5.$ -dimercapto-1,3-dithiole-2-thione, $e c d a^{2-}=1\cdot$ -ethoxycarbonyl1-cyanoethylene-2,2-dithiolate, $\operatorname{ET}\ =$ bis(ethylenedithio)tetrathiafulvalene, $\mathrm{C_{4}}\dot{\mathrm{S_{6}}}^{2-}=$ dithiolato, TTM-TTP $\mathbf{\varepsilon}=2{,}5$ -bis- $\cdot4^{\prime},5^{\prime}$ -bis(methylthio)- $^{1^{\prime},3^{\prime},}$ dithiol- $2^{\prime}$ -ylidene-1,3,4,6-tetrathiapentacene, $\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{5}=4{,}5$ -ethylenedithio-1,3-dithiole-2-thione, $\begin{array}{l l l}{(\mathbb{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{N})\mathrm{S}_{2}}&{=}&{2,2^{\prime}}\end{array}$ -dipyridyldisulfide, $4{\cdot}\mathrm{SpyH}\ =$ zwitterionic pyridium-4-thiolate. $a_{\ 303-393}\mathrm{~K~}$ . $\textit{b}_{300\mathrm{~K~}}$ and 1 bar. c Single crystal. d Four probe.
The proposed structures are based on various physicochemical studies and resemble those previously suggested for similar ligands but, as in the previous cases, there is unfortunately no $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray structure of these polymers. Basically, the polymeric structures of these heterobimetallic mixed ligands have the $\mathrm{{Cu(n)}}$ and $\mathrm{{Ni}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ in square planar geometry, Ag(I) in linear geometry and $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ , $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{I})}}$ and $\mathrm{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ are preferentially tetrahedrally coordinated.113
The electrical conductivity of these coordination polymers, measured between $300{-}393\mathrm{~K~}$ in pressed pellets using the two probe technique, shows low values of ca. $10^{-8}{-}10^{-9}\mathrm{S}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Doping the polymers with iodine produces an increase in their electrical conductivity by 1 or 2 orders of magnitude. The smaller conductivity values found in the undoped polymers are explained by the authors as a result of weaker S S, S Se and $\mathbf{M}{\cdot}\cdot\mathbf{\mathcal{S}}$ intermolecular interactions in the solid state.113
Following the idea of preparing new materials combining different physical properties such as conductivity and magnetism, R. Kanehama et al. have studied the reactions of $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega_{\mathrm{{II}}})}$ halides with bis(ethylenedithio)tetrathiafulvalene $\left(E T\right)^{109}$ (Fig. 19). They obtained new charge transfer coordination polymers of formula $(E T)\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}_{2}^{\mathrm{{I}}}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{4},(E T)_{2}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}_{6}^{\mathrm{{I}}}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{10}.$ , $(E T)_{2}[\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{I}}{}_{4}\mathrm{Br}_{6}E T]$ and $(E T)_{3}\mathrm{{Cu}_{\ 6}^{I}\mathbf{Br}_{10}(H_{2}O)_{2}}$ , where the $E T$ ligands coordinated to $\mathrm{{Cu(\mathrm{{I})}}}$ by the sulfur atoms of the ethylenedithio groups showing different coordination modes. Unfortunately, the reduction of the $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ ions to $\mathrm{{Cu(\mathrm{{I})}}}$ precludes the coexistence of magnetic and electrical properties in these compounds. The authors classified the compounds into three different groups according to the coordination modes of $E T$ molecules. Group I: the $E T$ coordinates as a monodentate ligand (the authors obtained discrete complexes with this mode of coordination). Group II: the $E T$ coordinates as trans-bidentate ligand. Group III: the $E T$ coordinates as cis-bidentate ligand. In the case of $(E T)\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{I}}{}_{2}\mathrm{Br}_{4}.$ a one-dimensional structure $[E T\mathrm{-BrCu-}(\upmu_{2}(\mathrm{Br})_{2}\mathrm{-Cu(Br)-}]_{n}$ is obtained, where the $E T$ ligands are coordinated to two copper ions with the two sulfur atoms in trans mode (Fig. 23). In $(E T)_{2}\mathrm{{Cu}^{I}{_{6}B r}_{10}}$ complex, all the $E T$ molecules coordinate to two copper ions with the two sulfur atoms in a cis-mode, giving a one dimensional chain running along the $c$ -axis (Fig. 24). Both structures present short interchain contacts established by S  S interactions.
The crystal structure of the $\mathrm{(ET)_{2}[C u_{4}B r_{6}E T]}$ polymer consists of two different parts. On one side the conducting $E T$ layers and, on the other, the $E T.$ -copper one-dimensional chains. In the $E T$ layers, the $E T$ molecules stack parallel to the $c$ -axis and are not coordinated to the copper ions. In the $E T$ -copper chains, the $E T$ molecules coordinate to two copper ions with the two sulfurs in a trans mode. Two quasi 1D copper complexes $\mathrm{\t{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{Br{\mathrm{-}}C u{\mathrm{-}}(\upmu_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{Br})_{2}{\mathrm{-}}C u(\mathbf{Br}){\mathrm{-}}}}\mathrm{]}_{n}$ bridged by $E T$ molecules form a ladder like structure (Fig. 25). This compound is a hybrid material in which conducting layers and a one-dimensional lattice coexist in the crystal packing.
In the complex $(E T)_{3}\mathrm{Cu}_{6}^{\mathrm{I}}\mathrm{Br}_{10}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}$ , the ET molecules coordinate to two Cu ions with the two S atoms in cis mode (Fig. 26).
The conductivity data obtained for these $E T$ coordination polymers, with the two-probe method on single crystals along the $c$ axis, using $15\upmu\mathrm{m}$ gold wires and carbon paste, show that these complexes are highly conducting materials (Table 11).
A very interesting work on 1D coordination polymers with dithio ligands has been reported by Szczepura et al.110 They prepared several 1D-coordination polymers by reaction of dithiolato $\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{6}{}^{2-}$ ligand with $\mathrm{[Ni(H_{2}O)_{6}]C l_{2}}$ or $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]B F_{4}}$ . The analysis of the insoluble black solids prompted the authors to propose the empirical formula $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}C_{4}S_{6}]}$ and $\mathrm{[NiC_{4}S_{6}]}$ with a 1D-polymeric structure. These polymers are diamagnetic and show semiconducting properties that have been attributed to the presence of a delocalized $\pi$ -electron system. Both $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}C_{4}S_{6}]}$ and $\mathrm{[NiC_{4}S_{6}]}$ behave as semiconductors with conductivity values of 0.1 and $0.9\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (measured at $294~\mathrm{K}$ , using the four contact method). Despite that the measurements were done in pressed pellets, these are some of the better values so far reported. However, to clarify this unusual conductivity their structures should be elucidated.
Following with the series of TTF derivatives, one dimensional homometallic silver(I) coordination polymers obtained with 2,5-bis- $\cdot4^{\prime},5^{\prime}$ -bis(methylthio)- $\cdot1^{\prime},3^{\prime}$ -dithiol- ${\cdot}2^{\prime}$ -ylidene-1,3,4,6-tetrathiapentacene (TTM-TTP) and 4,5-ethylenedithio-1,3-dithiole2-thione $\mathrm{(C_{5}H_{4}S_{5})}$ (Fig. 19) ligands have also been studied.104,114
In the first case, the one dimensional coordination polymer $\mathrm{\Delta[Ag(TTM-TTP)(CF_{3}S O_{3})]}$ (Fig. 27) is obtained by slow diffusion of $n$ -hexane into a solution containing a mixture of $\mathrm{AgCF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3}$ and TTM-TTP in acetonitrile/benzene. In this polymer, besides the $\mathrm{Ag(I)}{-}\mathrm{S}$ coordination bonds, there are extensive intermolecular $\mathbf{S\cdot\cdot}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{S}$ contacts, providing additional interactions across the individual chains.114
In the second case, the strong electron-donor $\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{5}$ ligand forms a one-dimensional silver(I) coordination polymer with two parallel coordinating molecular units (Fig. 28). The ligands coordinate to silver(I) through both sulfur atoms of the thiocarbonyl and through the sulfur atoms of the thioether. An additional extension of the contacts produces a 2D network by strong S S interactions. The iodine doped complex gives a room temperature conductivity of $3\times10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ measured in pressed pellets by a conventional two-probe method and a semiconductor behaviour.104 As in other already mentioned examples, the conductivity values of these two polymers also increase when doped with iodine.
Recently, the use of new synthetic routes as solvothermal (see Section 3.2 nucleobases as nitrogen ligands) and microwave methods has led to the synthesis of interesting coordination polymers with different architectures and dimensionalities. One of these examples was obtained by microwave solvothermal reaction of $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -dipyridyldisulfide and $\mathrm{Cu(BF_{4})_{2}}$ . The reaction proceeds with an unusual cleavage of $\mathbf{C}{-}\mathbf{S}$ and S–S bonds of the $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -dipyridyldisulfide ligand leading to a $\mathrm{{Cu(\mathrm{{I})}}}$ 1D-coordination polymer formation. The structure is generated based on the assembling of $\mathrm{Cu}_{9}$ cages (Fig. 29).111
The unusual polymeric structure presenting a large number of S bridges prompted the authors to measure its electrical conductivity. Studies carried out at variable temperature (Fig. 29), using the four probe technique, show a classical semiconductor behaviour with a high room temperature conductivity of 1.6  103 S cm1.111
Another polymer obtained under solvothermal conditions was synthesised by reaction of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dipyridyl disulfide (dpds), oxalic acid and CuI.112 The products obtained depend on the reaction time. Thus, three different $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{I}/\mathrm{S}$ -based coordination polymers were isolated: $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{6}(\upmu\mathrm{-}4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Spy}\mathrm{H})_{4}\mathrm{I}_{6}\right]_{n}.$ $\{\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(\upmu-I)}\cdot$ $\left(\upmu{-}4{-}\mathrm{SpyH}\right)_{3}]\mathrm{I}_{j n}^{\updownarrow}$ , and $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{5}(\upmu\mathrm{-}4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{SpyH})_{7}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{I})\mathrm{I}_{4}\right]_{n}$ .112
The process involves the in situ formation of the zwitterionic pyridinium-4-thiolate (4-SpyH) generated by S–S cleavage of the dpds ligand. The hexameric compound $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{6}(\upmu\mathrm{-}4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Spy}\mathrm{H})_{4}\mathrm{I}_{6}\right]_{n}$ forms a three dimensional diamond-like net, that will be discussed in Section 5.2. The structure of the dimeric compound $\{\lbrack\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\upmu-\mathrm{I})\cdot$ - $(\upmu\mathrm{-}4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{SpyH})_{3}]\updownarrow_{n}$ consists of a cationic $\{{[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{I})(\upmu\mathrm{-}4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{SpyH})_{3}]_{n}}\}^{n+}$ polymeric chain with disordered iodides embedded between the chains. In contrast, the pentameric compound $\mathrm{\Delta[Cu_{5}(\upmu\mathrm{-}4\mathrm{-}S p y H)_{7}.}$ $(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{I})\mathrm{I}_{4}]_{n}$ contains $[\mathrm{Cu}_{5}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{I})\mathrm{I}_{4}(\upmu\mathrm{-}4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Spy}\mathrm{H})_{7}]$ units that are interconnected with its neighbouring ones through two $\upmu{-}4{-}\mathrm{SpyH}$ bridges to form a neutral chain (Fig. 30). The formation of these three different compounds provided an interesting example that different coordination polymers could be produced from the same components under solvothermal conditions at different time periods, indicating that the often-neglected kinetic factors may be very important in this kind of synthesis.
The thermal variation (from 293 to $443~\mathrm{K}$ ) of the electrical conductivity measured for several single crystals of the dimeric and pentameric compounds with the two probe technique shows an exponential increase with increasing temperature from $6.84\times10^{-8}$ to $3.96\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ , for the dimeric $\{{\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(\upmu\mathrm{-}I)(\upmu\mathrm{-}4\mathrm{-}S p y H)_{3}]\mathrm{I}}}\}_{n}$ compound and from $2.74\times10^{-9}$ to $2.43\times10^{-7}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ , for the pentameric one, both displaying a typical semiconducting behaviour. The authors attributed the better conductivity of $\{{[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{I})(\upmu\mathrm{-}4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{SpyH})_{3}]\mathrm{I}}\}_{n}$ to the fact that the density of states or effective mass in this compound is larger than in the other one. In addition, the disordered counter iodides and the weak $\mathrm{Cu\cdot\cdot\cdotCu}$ interactions found in compound $\{\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(\upmu\mathrm{-}I)(\upmu\mathrm{-}4\mathrm{-}S p y H)_{3}]I\}_{n}$ may also contribute to the increase in conductivity.112
## 3.5. Systems containing metallomacrocycles
The metallomacrocycles such as metallophthalocyanines and metalloporphyrines have long been studied due to their interesting applications like dyes, pigments, catalysts, electrical conductors, etc. (Fig. 31).115
Their typical structures provide poor $\pi{-}\pi$ overlap between the adjacent molecules showing low electrical conductivities usually in the range $10^{-11}–10^{-13}$ $\mathrm{\Deltas~cm^{-1}}$ .116,117 Collecting these works, excellent reviews and articles about the electrical properties of these molecular materials have been published.118–120
In order to improve the electrical conductivity, new types of low-dimensional compounds have been prepared where the metallomacrocycles achieve a stacked co-facial arrangement with low interplanar distances allowing electron delocalization by $\pi{-}\pi$ overlapping. This can be achieved using several bridging ligands between the metallomacrocycles to generate a linear coordination polymer, where the central metal–axial ligand spine was expected to be a reasonable pathway for conduction.
Among the large variety of semiconducting polymers based on metallophthalocyanines, the most important families are the polyphthalocyaninato-metalloxanes $[{\mathrm{PcMO}}]_{n}$ $(\mathbf{M}~=~\mathrm{Si}$ Ge, Sn), studied in detail by Kenney and Marks,121–123 and those so-called ‘‘shish-kebab’’ developed by Hanack and Dini, based on the self-assembled Pc coordination polymers (Fig. 32).124
A significant amount of these kind of coordination polymers based on metallophthalocyanines and metalloporphyrines with several bridging ligands, $[\mathrm{MPc}/\mathrm{P}(\mathrm{L})]_{n}$ ( $\mathbf{P}\mathbf{c}\mathbf{s}\ =\ \$ phthalocyanines, $\textbf{P}=$ porphyrines; $\mathrm{~\bf~M~}=\mathrm{~\bf~Fe~}$ , Co, Mn, Ru, Os; and $\mathrm{~L~}=$ cyano, pyrazine, tetrazine and many others bidentate ligands), have been investigated as potential electrical conductors.125,126 These compounds showed low conductivity values in the lower part of the semiconducting region. However, doping these polymers with iodine increases conductivity up to values between $10^{-5}–10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . In contrast to other bridging ligands, for these compounds it seems that the use of tetrazine allows significant increases of the semiconducting properties without the need of external oxidative doping $(0.05–0.3\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ ).127 Table 12 shows a selection of these polymers with the most significant conductivity values.
Although functionalization leads to an increase in the solubility of the polymers, their electrical properties do not improve. Only a significant increase of the conductivity is observed by doping with lithium127 in both the monomeric (FePcOc) and polymeric $[\mathrm{FePcOc}(d i b)]_{n}$ ( $d i b=1,4$ -diisocyanobenzene) complexes (Table 12).
Therefore, despite the past and current interest in metallophthalocyanines and metalloporphyrines as conductive molecular materials,129 so far the electrical properties found in coordination polymers containing these molecules as buildingblocks have shown only moderate electrical conductivity values.
## 3.6. Aromatic hydrocarbons as linkers
Munakata et al. have been exploring the chemistry of polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons with different silver(I) metal salts for decades (Table 13). They have described a vast body of literature comprising a good number of structurally characterised frameworks. The first record considering electrical conduction properties came in $1997^{130}$ when they isolated two unusual silver(I)-aromatic networks containing pyrene $(p y r)$ and perylene (per) tectons, $[\mathrm{Ag}_{2}(p y r)(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}]$ and $[\mathrm{Ag}_{2}(p e r)(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}]$ respectively (Fig. 33), both displaying a tetra- $\boldsymbol{\upeta}^{2}$ coordination mode, linking four metal centres and rendering W-shaped polymeric chains and sheets, in this order.
a $b t p=1{,}2$ -benztriphenylene. $r u b=$ rubrene, bpyr $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ benzo[a]pyrene, $c o r= $ corenene, bphen $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ benzo[a]phenanthrene, dban $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ dibenz[a,h]- anthracene, ban $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ benz[a]anthracene, napyr $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ naphtho(2,3-a)pyrene, $b t p=1{,}2$ -benztriphenylene, dbchry $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ dibenzo[b,def]chrysene, bpry $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ benzo[e]pyrene, dpbd $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ trans,trans-1,4-diphenyl-1,3-butadiene, $b m s b=$ 1,4-bis(methylstyryl)benzene.
A compressed powder pellet with the standard two-probe method showed insulator behaviour for both species. However, $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ -doping resulted on semiconduction with $\sigma$ values at room temperature of $1.7~\times~10^{-5}$ and $4.4~\times~10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for $[\mathrm{Ag}_{2}(p y r)(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}]$ and $[\mathrm{Ag}_{2}(p e r)(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}]$ , respectively. These values were postulated to be connected with the presence of crystallised organic radicals, as evidenced by ESR spectroscopy.
In a later report, in 1998, the same authors succeeded in the construction of new polymeric forms of organosilver compounds with strained aromatic moieties.131 This time rubrene $(r u b)$ benzo[a]pyrene $(b p y r)$ and corenene (cor) were combined with ${\bf A}{\bf g}({\bf C}{\bf l O})_{4}{\cdot}{\bf H}_{2}{\bf O}$ to form novel polymeric networks formulated as: $\mathrm{\Delta[Ag_{4}(\ r u b)(C l O_{4})_{4}(H_{2}O)_{4}]}$ , $[\mathrm{Ag}_{4}(b p y r)_{2}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{4}(\mathrm{toluene})_{2}]$ and $[\mathrm{Ag}_{4}(c o r)_{3}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{4}]$ . The most significant features of the crystal structure of $\left[\mathrm{Ag}_{4}(r u b)(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}\right]$ are a 3D network consisting of twisted rubrene fragments coordinated by eight silver cations with both $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{n}}^{1}$ - and ${\mathfrak{n}}^{2}$ -bonding modes, loose of planarity of the rubrene fragment and lack of extended $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions (Fig. 34).
A single crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction study of $[\mathrm{Ag}_{4}(b p y r)_{2}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{4}.$ (toluene) $\mathbf{\tau}_{2}]$ revealed a double helical arrangement sandwiching a toluene solvent molecule, $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{n}}^{1}$ - and ${\mathfrak{n}}^{2}$ -coordination modes for silver cations and an intricate interweaved network of aromatic $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions (Fig. 35).
The solid-state structure of $[\mathrm{Ag}_{4}(c o r)_{3}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{4}]$ can be regarded as a triple-decker polymeric framework with both intra and intermolecular $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions warranting the formation of columnar stacks with very close contacts of $3.23\mathrm{~\AA~}$ between adjacent corenene planes (Fig. 36).
No doping was needed to observe conductivity and the recorded values measured at room temperature on a conventional two-probe method on pressed pellets were $1.9\times10^{-6}$ , $1.0\times10^{-5}$ and $3.1\times10^{-3}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}.$ for $\mathrm{\Delta[Ag_{4}(r u b)(C l O_{4})_{4}(H_{2}O)_{4}]}.$ $[\mathrm{Ag}_{4}(b p y r)_{2}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{4}(\mathrm{toluene})_{2}]$ and $[\mathrm{Ag}_{4}(c o r)_{3}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{4}]$ , respectively.
In 1999, Munakata et al. extended this family of conducting organosilver polymeric compounds with benzo[a]phenanthrene (bphen), dibenz[a,h]anthracene (dban), benz[a]anthracene (ban) and naphtho(2,3-a)pyrene (napyr) ligands.132 The reaction of these molecules with silver(I) salts generated herringbone-like architectures reminiscent of those of the free ligands. Thus, the crystalline solid state of $[\mathrm{Ag}_{3}(b p h e n)(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]$ consists of aromatic-linked chain strands along the $c$ axis where each ligand adopts di- $\cdot\boldsymbol{\eta}^{2}$ and tetra- $\cdot\boldsymbol{\eta}^{2}$ coordination modes bridging two and four metal centres, respectively. The existence of intermolecular $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions renders a supramolecular 2D sheet framework (Fig. 37).
$[\mathrm{Ag}_{2}(d b a n)(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}]$ exists in the solid state as a polymeric W-like sandwich of alternating $\mathrm{\bfAgClO_{4}}$ and dban buildingblocks repeating along the $b$ axis, each aromatic ring displays a tetra- $\cdot\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}^{2}$ coordination bridging four metal ions. Extended intermolecular $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions give rise to 2D sheets in the $a b$ plane (Fig. 38).
Similar multilayer 2D sheets are observed in the crystal structure of $\mathrm{\small{[Ag_{2}(}}b a n)(\mathrm{ClO_{4}})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\mathrm{]}$ where two independent silver ions are bonded to the head and tail of the aromatic rings and to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules. The perchlorate counterions exhibit mono and tridentate coordination modes and participate in silver coordination through $\mathbf{Ag-O-Cl-O-Ag}$ and $\mathbf{Ag-O-Ag}$ bonds (Fig. 39).
Although explicit details of electrical conduction for each polymer were not given, authors claimed that on a two-probe experiment with silver coated contacts the values observed for pressed pellets were in the range $1.65\times10^{-3}–6.9\times10^{-2}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ at room temperature.
A striking feature in the structure of $[\mathrm{Ag}_{2}(n a p y r)(\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3})_{2}]$ is the presence of an unprecedented $\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3}{}^{-}$ group tricoordinated to a silver(I) ion. The herringbone pattern of the free ligand is mainly retained giving rise to a 2D multilayer framework with aromatic sheets suspended between pairs of infinite ionic chains by $\mathrm{\bfAg-O-S-O-Ag}$ bonds (Fig. 40).
In a later communication, Munakata et al. reported a new metallocyclophane with a columnar aromatic stacking.133 This time the cyclophane motif was tethered through silver(I) ions rather than the previously reported C, O, S or N atoms, hence the unprecedented metallocyclophane nomenclature. When the T-shaped 1,2-benztriphenylene $(b t p)$ ligand reacts with $\mathrm{\bfAgClO_{4}}$ in $p$ -xylene, crystals with a $[\mathrm{Ag}_{0.5}(b t p)_{0.5}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{0.5}]$ formulation could be isolated and their $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray structure determined (Fig. 41).
The btp ligand shows a di- $\boldsymbol{\upeta}^{2}$ coordination and is $\pi{-}\pi$ faceto-face stacked to parent aromatic units by two bridging $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ centres. This repeating unit is further connected via $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ counterions affording a chain running along the $b$ axis. Additional intermolecular $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions provide columnar aromatic stacking of the argentocyclophane motifs. Noteworthy, the electrical conductivity of a compressed pellet of $[\mathrm{Ag}_{0.5}(b t p)_{0.5}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{0.5}]$ with a standard silver coated two-probe method revealed the highest value of all characterised organosilver complexes with $\sigma=1.32\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at room temperature.
In 2001 the same authors reported three new complexes of silver(I) with benzopyrene and two of them were found to be semiconductors.134 Single yellow crystals of $[\mathrm{Ag}_{2}(d b c h r y).$ $(\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3})_{2}][\mathrm{Ag}_{2}(\mathrm{toluene})_{2}(\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3})]_{n}$ were obtained combining $\mathrm{Ag}(\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3})$ and dibenzo $[b,d e f]$ ]chrysene (dbchry). Its crystal structure can be described as a 2D layer presenting cation– $\pi$ interactions of $\mathrm{{Ag(I)}}$ and dbchry together with a 1D polymer formed by $\mathrm{Ag(\mathrm{I})}$ and solvent toluene molecules (Fig. 42).
On the border limits of the scope of this review stands the stacking polymer formed by the reaction of $\mathrm{{Ag}(C l O_{4})}$ with benzo[e]pyrene $(b p y r)$ : $[\mathrm{Ag}_{4}(b p y r)_{4}(p$ -xylene) $\mathrm{(ClO_{4})_{4}]}$ . This polymer presents a tetranuclear core formed by two crystallographically different $\mathrm{{Ag(I)}}$ centres, two symmetry related bpyr molecules sandwiching a $p$ -xylene solvent molecule and two pairs of $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ counterions interacting via intermolecular $\pi{-}\pi$ contacts through extra pendant bpyr ligands, leading to a 2D sheet structure together with a 1D columnar stacking (Fig. 43).
The room temperature electrical conductivity values, measured with the two probe method, are $6.3~\times~10^{-2}~\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}$ in $[\mathrm{Ag}_{4}(b p y r)_{4}(p\mathrm{-xylene})(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{4}]$ and $5.8\ \times\ 10^{-2}\ \mathrm{~S~}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in $[\mathrm{Ag}_{2}(d b c h r y)(\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3})_{2}][\mathrm{Ag}_{2}(\mathrm{toluene})_{2}(\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3})]_{n}.$
Extending their postulate to multiphenyl dienes, Munakata et al. reported another semiconducting polymer in 2003.134 trans,trans-1,4-Diphenyl-1,3-butadiene (dpbd) was reacted with $\mathrm{\bfAgClO_{4}}$ to yield $[\mathrm{Ag(dpbd)(ClO_{4})}]$ as colourless crystals. Its structure shows a trigonal local environment for the silver atom formed by $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{n}}^{1}$ -bonds to two phenyl fragments and an oxygen atom from a $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}{}^{-}$ group. The dpbd coordinates two metal atoms building a 1D W-shaped infinite chain that stacks forming a herringbone (Fig. 44). Its electrical conductivity shows a room temperature $\sigma$ value of $2.1\times10^{-7}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ .
The last work published by Munaksta’s group concerning silver metal–organic frameworks relates to another multiphenyl diene ligand.135 The new complexes $[\mathrm{Ag}_{2}(b m s b)(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}]$ and $\mathrm{[Ag_{2}(}b m s b)(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}](\mathrm{BF}_{4})_{2}$ ( $\ b m s b=1,4$ -bis(methylstyryl)benzene) displayed $\sigma$ values of $1.0~\times~10^{-6}$ and $1.2\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}.$ respectively (Fig. 45 and 46). Attending to their crystal structure, the presence of columnar stacking in the latter but not in the former might be responsible for the enhanced electrical conductivity observed.
In the solid state structure of $\mathrm{[Ag_{2}(}b m s b)(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}](\mathrm{BF}_{4})_{2}$ (Fig. 45) the ligand face-to-face arrangement is secured via double silver $\upmu$ -tetra- $\cdot\boldsymbol{\upeta}^{2}$ bridges forming a metallocyclophane moiety with a chain structure. Strong contacts, both intra and interchain, generate a supramolecular 2D network.
On the other hand, a cyclophane motif is also observed for $[\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}_{2}(b m s b)(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{lO}_{4})_{2}]$ (Fig. 46). These cyclic structures give rise in the solid state to a supramolecular 2D polymer constructed by 1D chains where equivalent silver(I) centres bond to both phenyl and vinylene fragments of bmsb. The metal coordination sphere is completed with oxygen atoms of different $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ groups. Moreover, no $\pi{-}\pi$ intrachain interactions are observed whereas close interchain contacts of $3.42\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ between phenyl and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=C}$ are present.
## 4. Two dimensional coordination polymers
## 4.1. 2D polymers with inorganic linkers
Mixed-valence dinuclear species have proved to be resourceful building-blocks for the preparation of coordination polymers with notable electrical properties. Thus, Kawamura and colleagues have been exploiting the $[\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]$ (acam $\mathbf{\Sigma}=$ acetamidato) dimer in order to generate networks with different dimensionalities. In 2003 they constructed a 2D honeycomb-like frame employing $[\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}]$ as its neutral and radical cation $\mathrm{(Rh_{2}}^{4+}$ and $\mathrm{Rh}_{2}^{5+}$ ) forms linked by $\upmu_{3}–\mathrm{Cl}$ halides.136 The reaction between $[\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ or $[\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\mathrm{ClO}_{4}$ with excess NaCl gave $[\{{\bf R h}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}\}_{3}(\upmu_{3}{\bf-C l})_{2}]_{n}{\cdot}4n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Higher concentration of the radical salt resulted in the formation of the previously reported $[\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}\mathrm{Cl}]_{n}$ crystals (Fig. 11). The structure of $[\{{\bf R}{\bf h}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}\}_{3}({\upmu}_{3}{\bf-C l})_{2}]_{n}{\cdot}4n{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ consists of honeycomb layers in the $b c$ plane (Fig. 47). The layers are formed by hexagons containing six $[\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}]$ dimers in the sides and six $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ ions in the corners. Attending to the $\mathrm{Rh-O}$ and $\mathsf{R h{-}N}$ bond distances, there exists both $\mathrm{Rh}_{2}^{4+}$ and $\mathrm{Rh}_{2}^{5+}$ units in a $1:2$ ratio, giving rise to an average Rh2(14/3)+ o xidation state. Solvent water molecules are sandwiched between layers forming an intricate hydrogen-bond network. The authors postulated that these hydrogen bonds favour a localization in the metal’s oxidation state responsible for the low electrical conductivity of the network, with a value of $2\times10^{-7}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ measured on a pressed pellet at room temperature.
Following their studies with the $\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}$ building block, Fuma and Ebihara prepared in 2006 a 2D square-sheet comprising this unit linked with $\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}$ ions.137 This was the first example of a perrhenate ion bonded to four metal complexes. The structure of $[\{{\bf R h}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}\}_{2}(\upmu_{4}{\bf-R e O}_{4})]$ consists of equivalent $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Rh}}_{2}$ units linked by $\mathrm{ReO}_{4}{}^{-}$ ions as stacked square sheets in the bc plane. There are amido-NH and amido-O inter-layer hydrogen bonds (Fig. 48). The intra-dimer Rh–Rh distance is 2.4047(6) $\mathring\mathbf{A}$ . The network was assembled mixing equimolar amounts of $[\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $[\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]-$ $\mathrm{ClO_{4}}$ with excess $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{ReO}_{4}.$ , a common procedure for these type of arrays. The electrical conductivity of $[\{{\bf R}{\bf h}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}\}_{2}(\upmu_{4}{\bf-R}\mathrm{eO}_{4})]$ as a pressed pellet at room temperature was $8.9\times10^{-6}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Table 14).
## 4.2. 2D polymers with nitrogen-containing ligands
P. Amo-Ochoa et al. have synthesized a 2D multifunctional mixed-valence $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{I}/\mathrm{{II}}})}$ coordination polymer $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{Br}(\mathrm{IN})_{2}]_{n}$ $\mathbf{W_{\lambda}}=$ isonicotinate $\bf\Pi=\Pi\ p y{-}\bf{C O O}^{-}\}$ presenting conducting, magnetic and luminescent properties (Fig. 49).
Its structure consists of pairs of copper atoms bridged by two isonicotinato ligands and a bromide ion. The fourth coordination site of the copper ion is occupied by an additional terminal N-coordinated isonicotinato ligand. The geometry of this entity forms four-arm units which propagate along the bc plane in a 2D-framework (Fig. 49). Stacking of layers along the $a$ axis results in a 3D organization with $\pi$ -stacking between isonicotinato aromatic rings $(3.36\ \mathring\mathrm{A})$ . The electrical characterisation of the crystals, carried out by the two contacts method, showed semiconductor behaviour with a conductivity value of $1.2\times10^{-5}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at room temperature.
The singular crystal structure and its multifunctional features prompted the authors to exfoliate the crystals by means of ultrasounds. In such a way single-layers were isolated on mica and characterized by atomic force microscopy and XPS. This result suggests that 2D-coordination polymers can be considered as alternative materials to graphene.138
Rao et al. have studied supramolecular assemblies of cyanuric acid (CA) involving both hydrogen bonding and metal ion-coordination capabilities. Thus, during the course of their investigations, Rao et al. isolated a novel silver compound: $\mathrm{{\bfAg}}_{2}(\mathrm{CA})$ (Fig. 50), whose structure presents two dimensional $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ sheets with the cyanuric acid molecules in the interlayer space, forming linear hydrogen bonding.139
The presence of two-dimensional $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ sheets is expected to give rise to anisotropic conductivity. Accordingly, the values of the room temperature dc conductivity, parallel and perpendicular to the $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ sheets ( $b c$ plane) are ca. $5\times10^{-3}$ and ca. $2\times10^{-5}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ , respectively. The conductivity along the sheets is temperatureindependent down to $15\mathrm{K}$ (Table 15).
## 4.3. 2D polymers containing organosulfur ligands
As it has already been described (Section 3.4) the sulfur rich 4,5-ethylenedithio-1,3-dithiole-2-thione $\mathrm{(C_{5}H_{4}S_{5})}$ presents different available sites that can coordinate to metal ions. Furthermore, thanks to its capacity to act as an electron-donor, it has been considered as a good candidate for the synthesis of donor–acceptor metallic systems. As mentioned before (Section 3.4), Jie Dai et al. used this ligand to prepare 1D coordination polymers and, interestingly, a modification of the reaction conditions between ${\mathrm{C}}_{5}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}{\mathrm{S}}_{5}$ and $\mathrm{AgCF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3}$ lead to the synthesis of the 2D-coordination polymer $\mathrm{\small{[Ag(C_{5}H_{4}S_{5})C F_{3}S O_{3}]_{n}}}$ .104 In this polymer, there are two independent silver(I) atoms (types A and B), both showing a five coordinate environment. Type A silver atoms have a $\mathrm{S}_{4}\mathrm{O}$ donor set and are coordinated by four thiocarbonyl sulfur atoms from four different $\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{5}$ molecules and one oxygen atom from a triflate anion $\left(\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{-}\right)$ . Type B silver atoms are surrounded by a $\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ donor set from two thioether sulfur atoms and three oxygen atoms of triflate anions (Fig. 51).
$\mathrm{\Delta}[\mathrm{Ag}(\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{5})\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3}]_{n}$ presents short S S distances, indicative of an efficient intermolecular $\pi{-}\pi$ orbital interaction, usually related with an increase in the electrical conductivity. Since the mixed-valence oxidation state (partial-oxidation, reduction, or charge-transfer) is a key feature in many conducting compounds, this polymer was partially oxidized by iodinedoping. The studies of the electrical properties of this material showed that although the coordination polymer is an insulator with $\sigma<10^{-12}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ (measured in pressed pellets at $300~\mathrm{K}$ and using the two probe method, Table 16), the iodine-doped product behaves as a semiconductor with a room temperature conductivity of 1.5  104 S cm1.104
In an attempt to solve the insolubility problems found in metal thiolate compounds, some authors have used pyrimidyl nitrogen-containing thiols as ligands to generate crystalline networks with metal ions such as $\mathrm{\bfAg(\mathrm{{I})}}$ or $\mathrm{{Ni}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ . Two examples are reported in the literature with these kind of ligands.140 The first one describes a 2D-coordination polymer obtained by a solvothermal reaction between pyrimidine-2-thiol and ${\mathrm{Ni}}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}$ in $\mathrm{DMF/H_{2}O}$ .143 The lamellar structure of formula $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{S})_{4}$ (Fig. 52) presents electrical conductivity and ferromagnetic interactions between the $\mathrm{{Ni}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ centres. The conductivity measurement (in pressed pellets) of $5\times10^{-3}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ at room temperature increases upon heating, indicating typical semiconductor behaviour. The semiconducting properties may be attributed to the presence of an interconnected array of nickel(II) ions with pyrimidine presenting some $\mathrm{{Ni}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ -pyridine ring interactions.
The second example with a pyrimidyl nitrogen-containing thiol is an interesting 2D-polymer synthesized by Su et al.141 using pyridine-2-thiolate as building-block. This polymer of formula $\mathrm{[\{Ag(C_{5}H_{4}N S)\}]}_{n}$ $\mathrm{\underline{{C}}_{5}H_{4}N S}$ = pyridine-2-thiolate $(\mathrm{Py}\mathrm{S}^{-}))$ shows a graphite-like array of silver(I) ions (Fig. 53). Its structure consists of sheets where the silver atoms are connected by $\mathrm{Py}\mathrm{S}^{-}$ ligands to generate layers with the pyridyl groups of the $\mathrm{Py}\mathrm{S}^{-}$ ligands inserted into the interlayer region (the interlayer distance is $17.17\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . Each $\mathrm{Py}\mathrm{S}^{-}$ ligand acts as a $\upmu_{3}$ bridge to link two silver atoms through a S atom, and a third silver atom through a $\mathbf{N}$ atom. Each silver atom is coordinated by two S atoms and one $\mathbf{N}$ atom. The electrical conductivity measured in a pressed pellet is 2.04 105 S cm1 at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , with a semiconducting behaviour that was attributed to the presence of Ag–Ag interactions.141
On a subsequent study, two years later Su et al. described different coordination modes of the pyridine-2-thiolate (and/or thione) (Spy and/or HPyS, respectively) upon silver(I) complexation.144 Several polymers were described and $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction analyses of all compounds were collected.
Although precise data on their electrical conductivity were not provided, the $\sigma$ values found were typical of insulators, with values in the range $10^{-12}$ to $10^{-15}\mathrm{{S}\ c m^{-1}}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
Soaking $\mathrm{[\{Ag(C_{5}H_{4}N S)\}]_{\it n}}$ in DMF for one month gave rise to the isomer $\mathrm{[\{Ag_{6}(C_{5}H_{4}N S)_{6}\}]_{\it n}}$ . In the absence of water and using MeCN rather than DMF, $\mathrm{[\{Ag(C_{5}H_{5}N S)_{2}\}(B F_{4})_{2}]_{\it n}}$ was obtained. In the same way $\mathrm{[\{Ag(C_{5}H_{5}N S)_{2}\}(C l O_{4})_{2}]_{\it n}}$ was synthesised from $\mathrm{\bfAgClO_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{[\{Ag_{4}(C_{5}H_{5}N S)_{6}\}(N O_{3})_{4}]_{\mathfrak{n}}}$ from ${\bf A g N O}_{3}$ . The $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ salts are best described as isostructural cationic metal–metal-chains whereas the ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ salt forms a 1D pseudo-ladder chain (Fig. 54).
Another layered polymer was isolated from the reaction between pyridine-2-thiol $({\mathrm{HPyS}})$ and $\mathrm{\bfAgBF_{4}}$ in $\mathrm{MeOH:CH_{3}C N\ (40:1)}$ , in this way $[\{\mathrm{Ag}_{5}(\mathrm{Spy})_{4}(\mathrm{HPy}\mathrm{S})\}\mathrm{BF}_{4}]_{n}$ was assembled (Fig. 55).
The reaction of ${\bf K}_{2}(i{\mathrm{-mnt}})$ with HPyS and ${\bf A g N O}_{3}$ furnished the polymeric chain $[\mathrm{Ag}_{4}(\upmu_{4}\mathrm{-}i\mathrm{-}\mathrm{mnt})_{2}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{HPy}\mathrm{S})_{2}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{HPy}\mathrm{S})_{4/2}]_{n}$ (Fig. 56). Finally, when HPyS, $\mathrm{NaS}_{2}\mathrm{CNEt}_{2}$ and ${\bf A g N O}_{3}$ were combined another polymeric 1D species resulted, $[\mathrm{Ag}_{4}(\upmu_{3}-\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{CNEt}_{2})_{2}(\upmu_{2}-\mathrm{SPy})_{4/2}]_{n}$ (Fig. 56).
Recently, Che et al. have reported an unprecedented 2-D $\mathrm{{Cu-S}}$ coordination polymer formed by self-assembly of $\mathrm{{Cu(\mathrm{{I})}}}$ ions with 4-hydroxythiophenol. Its X-ray structure solved by using powder X-ray diffraction data agrees with the lamellar architecture of CuHT $\mathrm{{HT}=4}$ -hydroxythiophenolate). The self-assembled coordination network displays ionic behaviour with a bulk electrical conductivity value measured on pellets at room temperature of 120 S cm1.142
## 5. Three dimensional coordination polymers
## 5.1. 3D coordination polymers with inorganic linkers
In a short communication in 2004, Kawamura et al. reported the isolation of a 3D diamond-like network built with $\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}$ paddle-wheel complexes connected via $\upmu_{4}$ -iodide bridges.145 The slow evaporation of an aqueous solution with $[\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ $\mathrm{[Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\mathrm{ClO}_{4}$ and NaI produced the $\mathrm{[\{Rh_{2}(a c a m)_{4}\}_{2}I]_{{n}}.6n H_{2}O}$ network (Fig. 57).
X-Ray diffraction analyses revealed the presence of equivalent $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Rh}}_{2}$ and $\upmu_{4}{-}\mathrm{I}$ units arranged on a diamond framework with water solvent molecules occupying the interstitial holes. There are inter-dimer hydrogen bonds formed between all amido-NH and amido-O that extend to the co-crystallised water molecules. Interestingly, the solvent molecules play a key role in the electrical conductivity of the polymer, a pressed pellet of $\mathrm{[\{Rh_{2}(a c a m)_{4}\}_{2}I]_{\it{n}}{\cdot}6n H_{2}O}$ displayed a room temperature electrical conductivity of $1.4\ \times\ 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{~S~}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ that decreases to $7.0\times10^{-9}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ when the sample dehydrates (Table 17). Exposing compound to moisture resulted in both the recovery of the $[\{\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(a c a m)_{4}\}_{2}\mathrm{I}]_{n}{\cdot}6n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ formulation, as inferred by $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ -ray powder diffraction analyses, and an upturn in the electrical conductivity which scopes over a range of $10^{5}$ during dehydration–rehydration cycling. The authors claimed that the diamond structure persisted over those cycles.
## 5.2. 3D coordination polymers with organic linkers
One of the most notable examples of conductivity in coordination polymers are the compounds $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathbf{R}^{1},\mathbf{R}^{2}{\-}\mathbf{-DCNQI})_{2}$ where ${\bf R}^{1}$ , $\mathbf{R}^{2}\ =\ \mathrm{~H~}$ , Cl, Br, I, Me, OMe and $\mathbf{DCNQI}\ =$ $N,N^{\prime}$ -dicyanoquinonediimine. Their structures contain seven interpenetrating diamond nets, with infinite stacks of DCNQI radical anions (Fig. 58). The copper atoms have an average oxidation state of $+1.33$ and the series show metal-like conductivities. The dimethyl derivative, for example, has a conductivity of 103 S cm1 at room temperature.146
As we already mentioned in Section 3.4, the solvothermal synthetic methods may give rise to new interesting supramolecular structures. For example, reaction of $\mathrm{CuI}$ with $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dipyridyl disulfide (dpds) and oxalic acid at three different time periods allowed the isolation of three $\mathrm{{Cu/I/S}}$ -based coordination polymers, $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{6}(\upmu\mathrm{-}4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Spy}\mathrm{H})_{4}\mathrm{I}_{6}\right]_{n}.$ , $\{[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{I})(\upmu\mathrm{-}4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Spy}\mathrm{H})_{3}]\mathrm{I}\}_{n}$ , and $[\mathrm{Cu}_{5}(\upmu\mathrm{-}4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{SpyH})_{7}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{I})\mathrm{I}_{4}]_{n}^{\ 112}$ (the two last ones are one dimensional polymers that were already described in Section 3.4). The first complex $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{6}(\upmu{-}4{-}\mathrm{Spy}\mathrm{H})_{4}\mathrm{I}_{6}\right]_{n}$ has an adamantine-type $\mathrm{Cu}_{6}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ core. The four equatorial $\mathrm{{Cu(\mathrm{{I})}}}$ atoms are tetrahedrally coordinated by two bridging $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ atoms and two S atoms from two $4{\cdot}\mathrm{SpyH}$ ligands while the two apical $\mathrm{{Cu(\mathrm{{I})}}}$ centres are trigonally coordinated by one terminal iodide and two sulfur atoms from one 4-SpyH ligand. Each $\mathrm{[Cu_{6}S_{4}]}$ core acts as a tetrahedral 4-connecting node and is linked with four other equivalent $\mathrm{[Cu_{6}S_{4}]}$ cores through four pairs of iodide bridges coordinated to the equatorial $\mathrm{{Cu(\mathrm{{I})}}}$ ions, forming a 3D diamond-like network (Fig. 59). All these features suggest that this 3D network may not be robust enough to survive at solvothermal conditions and may dissociate into other more stable species if the time of this reaction is deliberately prolonged. The conductivity of the crystal increased exponentially from $1.97\times10^{-9}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ to $6.32\times10^{-7}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ , upon heating from 293 to $443\mathrm{~K~}$ .
Cao et al. reported in 2002 a novel 3D polymer constructed from silver(I) metal centres and benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{3}b t c)$ .147 Slow diffusion of methanolic ${\bf A g N O}_{3}$ over aqueous $\mathrm{H}_{3}b t c$ gave rise to the formation of the polymeric $[\{\mathrm{Ag}(\mathrm{H}_{2}b t c)_{2}\}\{\mathrm{Ag}_{2}(\mathrm{H}b t c)\}]_{n}$ compound. Its crystal structure shows the existence of two interpenetrated distinct building blocks: $\mathrm{Ag}_{8}(\mathrm{H}b t c)_{12/3}$ (A unit) and $\mathrm{Ag}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}b t c)_{2}$ (B unit) (Fig. 60). The intricate structure can be rationalised as distorted ladder-like chains of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ atoms (Fig. 61) bridged by both $\mathrm{H}_{2}b t c^{-}$ and $\mathrm{H}b t c^{2-}$ spacers. Remarkably, the whole structure is sustained by direct $\mathbf{Ag-Ag}$ interactions.
The electrical conductivity, measured on pressed pellet showed a $\sigma$ value of $1.06\times10^{-6}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at room temperature that increased with increasing temperature in a semiconducting regime.
In 2004, the group of $\mathrm{w}_{\mathrm{u}}$ described a new 3D silver organodiphosphonate framework: $\mathrm{[Ag_{4}(O_{3}P C H_{2}C H_{2}P O_{3})]}$ ,148 with a zeolite-like structure sustaining a sub-lattice with direct $\mathbf{Ag-Ag}$ contacts (Fig. 62). The electrical conductivity of a polycrystalline pellet showed maximum values at $170~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ of ca. $7\times10^{-7}$ (Fig. 63).
Table 18 collects the conductivity data reported on 3D-CPs with organic linkers.
## 6. Conclusions and perspectives
In the past few decades, the synthesis and characterisation of low-dimensional crystalline conducting materials has attracted much interest due to their highly tuneable structures and their potential applications in electronics and optoelectronic devices.149
Coordination polymers are currently one of the hottest topics in Inorganic and Supramolecular Chemistry (see for instance the excellent review by C. Janiak that is now receiving over 200 citations a year and the special volume devoted in this journal to MOFs).150 There is no doubt of the technological impact expected for coordination polymers within the next coming years, in fact some of them are already being used in industrial applications.
Despite the electrical conductivity not being the prime focus of research, several conductive coordination polymers have been reported in the last few decades. We have shown that this tendency seems to be changing due to the current interest in the development of highly conducting coordination polymers aimed for novel technological applications: porous electrode for batteries, fuel cells, capacitors, sensors, nanomaterials, etc.6,7,151 Thus, from the pioneering works of Interrante et al. in the 70’s on the MX chains and of Bellitto et al. in the 80’s on the MMX chains, the number of conducting CPs of these and other families has shown an exponential increase with time. This huge rise is the logical consequence of the increase in the number of used metals and ligands (either as linkers or as chelating units). Thus, besides the initial examples with Pt, Pd and Ni, here we have seen that many other metals such as $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ , Rh, Ru, Cu, Au, Fe,. . . can also be used to prepare CPs. The number of bridging ligands has also increased from the initial simple halides to more complex ones such as $\mathrm{CN^{-}}$ , ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{SCN^{-}}$ , $\mathsf{S e C N}^{-}$ , ${\bf N}_{3}^{-}$ , pyrazines, nucleobases, diverse S-containing ligands and aromatic hydrocarbons,. . . The chelating ligands stabilising the structure and completing the coordination environment of the metal atoms have also increased from the initial alkyldiamines (en, tn, bn and chxn) and carboxylates to more complex ones such as dithiomalonamide, thioureas, thioamides, dibenzyl sulfide, diphosphites, dithiocarboxylates, acetamides, TTF-type donors, dithiolates,. . .
In this review we have tried to summarize the state-of-the-art in this topic collecting the vast number of compounds structurally and electrically characterized that were disperse in the literature (in some cases there were no clear relationship between the reported examples). This compilation has also shown a large dispersion of the experimental conductivity values (from highly conducting CPs with room temperature conductivities above $10^{2}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ to very poor semiconductors with room temperature conductivities below $10^{-14}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1},$ . The experimental setups and conditions in the conductivity measurements are also very disperse since they depend in many cases on the availability of large enough single crystals and on the conductivity values and thermal behaviours. Thus, there are conductivity measurements made on single crystals, pressed pellets, thin films or even nanowires with just a few molecular chains. The thermal variations cover a wide range of temperatures from liquid helium to values well above room temperature (even above $440\mathrm{K}$ ) and there are also measurements done with different applied pressures up to 250 kbar. Finally, although this is not expected to have an influence on the reported values since the resistivities are usually quite high, there are measurements done with the two and four contact methods. All these experimental differences have also to be taken into account when comparing values and behaviours of the electrical conductivity in these CPs. More additional experimental studies need to be done. Indeed this is necessary to gain understanding towards the redesign of better conductors. In any case the aspects collected here will need a further revision with additional new incoming experiments.
Moreover, the data currently available suggest that the number of electrical conductive 1D coordination polymers is higher than 2D- and 3D-CPs. This is probably motivated by the interest in the preparation of low-dimensional materials but in fact at this point we cannot conclude about whether this is just experimental evidence or real data. We believe that along the next years the number of CPs will not depend on the dimensionality but rather on other more important aspects related with the structure and composition.
Despite the dispersion of data reported, some insights can be inferred. Thus, we and others agree that the mixed-valence oxidation state of the metal ions seems to affect the electronic aspects including electrical conductivity. Probably one of the most relevant examples in CPs is the case of the MMX chains with platinum and dithiocarboxylate linkers.
The metal-to-metal direct interactions are important but not essential for conduction. In this respect it is obvious that short metal-to-metal distances may give rise to high conductivity when the metals are close enough to form a metal–metal bond. Nevertheless, when the metal–metal distance increases, the conductivity decreases but does not vanish completely.
Other aspects concerning the specific use of metal ions and ligands in the design of CPs are of high relevance in terms of conductivity. In fact the experimental data already available suggest that CPs containing metals such as platinum, nickel, silver and copper combined with sulfur- and halides-containing ligands may be a good strategy.
Few theoretical calculations have been used to explain experimental results and conducting mechanism.1,83,88,100–102 Albeit, we need to go one step forward in order to predict successful strategies leading to the design of new CPs.
